\begin{abstract}
  % Among the various modalities of information present in the real world, auditory perception is an indispensable part of human experience, and music constitutes a significant component of human life. In the realm of multimedia, a vast array of content such as images, videos, and text often comes accompanied by music. With the advancement of multi-modal technologies, the barriers between these forms of content and audio are being dismantled. Consequently, multi-modal music generation holds vast potential for application and development. Moreover, multi-modal information can enrich the music generation process, guiding models to produce more diverse and creative musical pieces. As a result, multi-modal music has emerged as a hot research topic in the fields of multi-modal studies and music generation. However, this nascent field still faces numerous challenges, such as how to integrate and utilize multi-modal information to guide music generation, how to achieve alignment of multi-modal data, the creation of datasets and evaluation methods for multi-modal music generation tasks, and issues surrounding the originality of multi-modal music generation. This article provides a comprehensive review of multi-modal music generation, categorizing existing technologies based on different guidance modalities and models. It also introduces current multi-modal music datasets and evaluation methods for multi-modal music generation. Finally, the article discusses the current challenges and future prospects in the development of multi-modal music generation. 

 % This survey explores the evolution of music generation from single-modal to cross-modal and multi-modal approaches, driven by advancements in AI and deep generative models. We focus on the interaction of various modalities, including audio, symbolic music, text, images, and video, in guiding music generation.  The paper reviews existing methods, datasets, and evaluation metrics, highlighting the challenges of integrating diverse modalities.  We also discuss modality representation techniques crucial for effective music understanding and generation, emphasizing the need for specific modality encoding models. Finally, we provide the future research direction in multi-modal music generation.

 % Multi-modal music generation, using multiple modalities like text, images, and video alongside musical scores and audio as guidance, is an emerging research area with broad applications. This paper reviews this field, categorizing music generation systems from the perspective of modalities. It covers modality representation, multi-modal data alignment, and their utilization to guide music generation. 
 % % We also discuss current datasets and evaluation methods. Key challenges in this area include effective multi-modal integration, large-scale comprehensive datasets, and systematic evaluation methods. Finally, we provide an outlook on future research directions focusing on creativity, efficiency, multi-modal alignment, and evaluation.
 % The discussion also covers current datasets and evaluation methods. Key challenges in this area include effective multi-modal integration, large-scale comprehensive datasets, and systematic evaluation methods. Future research directions focus on creativity, efficiency, multi-modal alignment, and evaluation.
 Multi-modal music generation, using multiple modalities like text, images, and video alongside musical scores and audio as guidance, is an emerging research area with broad applications. This paper reviews this field, categorizing music generation systems from the perspective of modalities. The review covers modality representation, multi-modal data alignment, and their utilization to guide music generation. Current datasets and evaluation methods are also discussed. Key challenges in this area include effective multi-modal integration, large-scale comprehensive datasets, and systematic evaluation methods. Finally, an outlook on future research directions is provided, focusing on creativity, efficiency, multi-modal alignment, and evaluation.
\end{abstract}

