Recently, some studies have explored the generation of music based on dance videos. Dance music generation requires multi-dimensional alignment between music and dance in rhythm, style, emotion, and other aspects. % For rhythmic alignment, Su et al.~\cite{su2021rhythmic} proposed RhythmicNet. RhythmicNet employs a three-stage pipeline including Video2Rhythm, Rhythm2Drum and Drum2Music. But it focuses on symbolic music and lacks timbral diversity. Thus, Zhu et al.~\cite{zhu2022d2mgan} proposed D2M-GAN, which uses Music VQ-VAE~\cite{dhariwal2020jukebox} for rich audio synthesis. For visual encoding, D2M-GAN combines 3D Skinned Multi-Person Linear model (SMPL) keypoints~\cite{loper2015smpl} and I3D features~\cite{carreira2017i3d} to encode both motions and visual frames. To reduce data dependency, Li et al. (2024) proposed a Dual-Path Rhythm-Genre Inversion method, integrating pretrained text-to-music models with rhythm/style encoders for dance-to-music generation using minimal data. You et al. introduced MoMu-Diffusion, combining motion-to-music and music-to-motion generation through BiCoR-VAE (contrastive alignment) and a diffusion transformer (DiT), enabling joint generation via cross-guidance sampling.
