Li et al.~\cite{li2024dance} developed an encoder-based textual inversion technique that integrates rhythm and style information from dance videos into pre-trained text-to-music models. Their Dual-Path Rhythm-Genre Inversion method extracts rhythmic patterns and stylistic elements via specialized encoders, mapping these features to text embeddings while dynamically adjusting pseudo-word representations to enable precise alignment with diverse dance movements.
By incorporating a pre-trained music generation model, the data volume required for training is reduced to approximately 1\% of that needed for training from scratch. % Their approach, termed Dual-Path Rhythm-Genre Inversion, introduces both a rhythm encoder and a style encoder to extract rhythmic sequences and stylistic features from dance videos, respectively, and maps these extracted features into a text embedding space. Specifically, the rhythm encoder extracts rhythm information by analyzing the 2D keypoint movements of dancers and calculating velocity and acceleration. The style encoder, conversely, utilizes one-hot encoding to represent dance styles, mapping these representations to text embeddings. Distinct from traditional text inversion methods, this technique achieves precise control over the generated music through the dynamic adjustment of pseudo-word text embeddings, thereby enabling adaptation to rhythmic variations across different videos.

However, generating music for dance videos is still somewhat distinct from generating music for general videos. % Expanding beyond dance, LORIS (Yu J et al.) uses a Latent Conditional Diffusion Model with Hawkes process-enhanced rhythm encoding for sports videos. CMT (Shangzhe Di et al.) generates symbolic background music using video-derived rhythm features but underutilizes visual semantics. V-MusProd (Le Zhuo et al.) advances this by incorporating semantic and color features into a three-stage Transformer model (chords, melody, accompaniment) for thematic alignment. Despite progress, video-to-background-music generation still lacks models that holistically interpret visual content. Future work requires unified frameworks capable of leveraging comprehensive video understanding for emotionally and contextually coherent music generation.
Yu et al.~\cite{yu2023loris} proposed the LORIS framework, broadening the applicability of models from dance to diverse sports scenarios, such as gymnastics and figure skating. LORIS is a generative framework based on a latent conditional diffusion model~\cite{rombach2022ldm,karras2022edm}, achieving long-term music generation synchronized with the video rhythm. Specifically, LORIS initially extracts visual and rhythmic information from videos through a context-aware conditional encoder. Within the encoder, visual information is captured for temporal dependencies using a pre-trained I3D network~\cite{carreira2017i3d} and bidirectional LSTM~\cite{hochreiter1997lstm}, while rhythmic information is extracted from motions through an improved rule-based method. Subsequently, these multi-modal information streams are integrated into a latent diffusion model via a cross-modal attention mechanism. The framework further employs the Hawkes process~\cite{hawkes1971spectra,mei2017neuralhawkes,zhang2020selfhawkes} to enhance positional encoding of rhythmic points, ensuring a high degree of consistency between the generated music and the video rhythm. Nevertheless, this work remains confined to videos of human motions.
% In the broader domain of video background music generation, Di et al. introduced the Controllable Music Transformer (CMT)~\cite{di2021cmt}. This model can generate corresponding symbolic music based on the rhythm of the video. In CMT, the researchers proposed an algorithm to compute video timing, motion speed, and motion saliency within videos, correlating these with beat, note density, and note strength in music, respectively. Ultimately, CMT performs controllable symbolic music generation based on these note attributes derived from videos, as well as user-specified instrument and style information, resulting in background music for videos. 
Di et al.~\cite{di2021cmt} introduced the Controllable Music Transformer (CMT), which generates symbolic music that aligns with video rhythm. CMT establishes correlations between video elements and musical attributes. By incorporating these video-derived attributes along with user-specified instrument and style preferences, it produces customized background music for videos.
However, CMT extracts and utilizes only rhythmic information from videos, representing a limited fraction of the information contained within videos. 
To advance research in video background music generation, Zhuo et al.~\cite{zhuo2023video} proposed a comprehensive solution encompassing a dataset, benchmark model, and evaluation metrics. The researchers presented a Transformer-based three-stage generative model, V-MusProd, sequentially generating chords, melodies, and accompaniment.  Beyond referencing the methods in CMT to control music rhythm using motion features, V-MusProd also extracts semantic and color information from videos to generate music consistent with the video themes and emotions. % In comparison to CMT, V-MusProd utilizes more visual information provided in videos, thus exhibiting improved consistency with videos.  
However, V-MusProd still exhibit limitations in fully utilizing video information. Video background music generation necessitates a unified model capable of comprehensively understanding complete videos and generating corresponding music. This calls for multi-modal generative models, which will be detailed in Section~\ref{sec:multi-modal}.

