\begin{abstract}
    \textbf{Purpose:} Little research has explored the communication needs of autistic adults and how their needs differ from those of other disabled populations. Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) can support these communication needs, but more guidance is needed on how to design AAC to support this population.

    \textbf{Materials and Methods:} We conducted an online, asynchronous, text-based focus group with five autistic adults to explore their social communication and community engagement and how AAC can help support them.

    \textbf{Results and Conclusion:} Our analysis of the participant responses found that 1) participants' emotional experiences impacted the communication methods they used, 2) speaking autistic adults can benefit from AAC use, and 3) autistic shutdown creates dynamic communication needs. We present implications for future AAC design: supporting communication in times of shutdown, indicating communication ability to communication partners, and a need to better understand the fear of using AAC. These implications can inform the design for future AAC systems. We also provide themes for future autism research: exploring the impact of a late diagnosis, gaining a better understanding of the communication needs during autistic shutdown, and expanding research to include the social and environmental factors that impact communication. Finally, we provide guidance on how future online focus groups can be run in an accessible manner.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{autism, asynchronous focus group, social communication, community engagement, augmentative and alternative communication (AAC)}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention estimates that 1 in 36 children has an Autism Spectrum Disorder diagnosis \cite{maenner_prevalence_2023}. Autism is characterized, in part, by a disability in social communication and interaction \cite{american_psychiatric_association_diagnostic_2022}. This communication disability can manifest differently across autistic people.  Some use speech as their primary means of communication, while others speak only rarely or not at all (i.e., nonspeaking). Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) can be used to support the communication needs of autistic people \cite{ganz_aac_2015}, especially those who are nonspeaking \cite{tager-flusberg_minimally_2013}. However, only a small portion of research on AAC and autism has included adults. \citet{holyfield_systematic_2017} found that only 6 of the 18 studies they identified had adult participants and none focused on social communication and community engagement.

Autistic adults, including those who use speech, can benefit from using AAC. However, they can face barriers that prevent them from using AAC, such as a lack of knowledge about the existence of AAC or believing that it is only for nonspeaking people~\cite{zisk_augmentative_2019}. There is a need to conduct more research with autistic adults on their communication needs and how AAC can support those needs, as shown by~\citet{holyfield_systematic_2017} and called for by~\citet{zisk_augmentative_2019}. Additionally, there is a need to research supporting communication beyond that required to meet basic wants and needs, as called for by \citet{holyfield_systematic_2017} and \citet{light_changing_2012, light_communicative_2014}.

Given the lack of exploration of this area, we conducted a focus group with autistic adults with the goal of answering the following research questions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item How do autistic adults define and engage with their community?
    \item How do autistic adults' current communication strategies impact their engagement?
    \item What do autistic adults want to see in future communication supports?
    \item How can focus groups be made accessible to AAC users and autistic participants?
\end{enumerate}

In this study, we conducted an online, asynchronous focus group to capture the lived experiences of autistic adults as they engaged with their communities using their current communication methods. We asked participants questions about their current communication strategies, how these strategies help or hinder their community engagement, how their communication style changes based on the surrounding context, and what they wish others knew or did when communicating with autistic adults. Our analysis of their posts and replies identified overarching themes stemming from participants' internal emotional state, past interpersonal experiences, and external factors such as the actions of communication partners.

We found that, while many of our participants used speech to communicate, there was a desire but a fear of introducing part-time AAC use into their daily lives. We also found that autistic adults can have dynamic communication needs stemming from the shared experience of autistic shutdown. The participants suggested that AAC systems may need to provide more focused support during these times of shutdown to help communicate their current abilities to communication partners. We show there is a need for more research exploring the social and environmental aspects of AAC and suggest themes for future AAC design research. Finally, we provide guidance on how to make online, asynchronous focus groups more accessible for autistic adults and AAC users.

\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Social Communication and Community Engagement}

Humans are relational creatures who want to form connections with other people \cite{baumeister_need_1995}. These connections typically occur through communication in focused interactions~\cite{goffman_behavior_1963}. People will make efforts to control how they present themselves and are perceived by others, creating different images of themselves like characters in a performance~\cite{goffman_presentation_1959}. This includes how they communicate with others. When communicating in social settings, people try to build common ground~\cite{kashima_grounding_2077} and use the physical and psychological context to ground the interaction~\cite{snyder_interpersonal_2007}. Such communication strategies help to foster relationships between people and create a sense of community.

Community is built on social interaction~\cite{hoffer_understanding_1931}, which may occur between a few or many individuals, resulting in communities of varying sizes. These communities, or social groups, tend to form through working towards a common goal~\cite{hiller_community_1941}. These attributes led~\citet{cohen_introduction_1990} to define community as ``a specific group of people who reside in a distinct geographical area, share a common culture, and are capable of acting collectively towards a given goal''. However, in the age of social media, a community can transcend geographical boundaries~\cite{gruzd_enabling_2013}. Online communities form around a common culture and are capable of acting collectively towards a common goal.

\subsection{Autism and Social Communication}

Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is characterized, in part, by deficits in social communication~\cite{american_psychiatric_association_diagnostic_2022}. As such, there has been much research on teaching social norms and communication to autistic children and young adults~\cite{gates_efficacy_2017, laugeson_abcs_2014, laugeson_randomized_2015, watkins_review_2015}. However as discussed by~\citet{zisk_augmentative_2019}, little work has been done with autistic adults and their communication support needs. This lack of support can have emotional impacts; for example, autistic adults have been shown to have higher levels of loneliness compared to neurotypical adults~\cite{ee_loneliness_2019, petty_blog-based_2023}.

One technique autistic people use to try and fit in with neurotypical people is \textit{masking}. When autistic individuals mask, they try to suppress the neurodivergent aspects of themselves to appear more like neurotypical people~\cite{miller_masking_2021}. They often mask to combat the stigma that comes with being disabled~\cite{alaghband-rad_camouflage_2023, hull_putting_2017}. Controlling one's presentation of self can help disabled people develop a sense of community where it might otherwise be lacking. Being disabled can drastically impact one's ability to engage with others~\cite{goffman_stigma_1963}, leading to increased feelings of loneliness, anger, stress, and anxiety~\cite{cacioppo_loneliness_2006}. However, autistic people still desire to be known and understood as they are, not as their masked selves~\cite{franz_i_2018}.

\subsection{Augmentative and Alternative Communication}

Augmentative and alternative communication (AAC) addresses the needs of people with speech and communication disabilities using a range of tools, techniques, and supports \cite{beukelman_augmentative_2020}. AAC can be broadly organized into two categories: unaided and aided. Unaided AAC is where someone makes use of only their body and does not use external tools. This includes techniques such as signing, gestures, facial expressions, and other forms of movement. Aided AAC makes use of external tools. These tools can be low-tech tools (i.e., tools that do not use electronics-based technology), such as pen and paper or a communication book, or high-tech tools like speech-generating devices and software applications.

The advent of mobile technology has helped high-tech AAC grow in availability and power, providing more opportunities to support communication~\cite{mcnaughton_ipad_2013}. However, communication support decisions are sometimes based solely on the technology available, with little regard for the needs and preferences of the individual being served. It is important to keep the communication needs of the AAC user as the primary focus during the AAC intervention process~\cite{mcnaughton_building_2019, mcnaughton_ipad_2013, light_putting_2013}. As the field of AAC grows, so does the scope of communication needs that can be supported through AAC use~\cite{light_changing_2012}.

Introducing AAC can be beneficial for autistic people.~\citet{mirenda_autism_2013} found that AAC can increase speech in young autistic children and also reduce their amount of long-term AAC use. However,~\citet{ganz_aac_2015}, \citet{ganz_meta-analysis_2012}, and \citet{holyfield_systematic_2017} show that few studies have explored the impact AAC can have on the communication of autistic adults.~\citet{zisk_augmentative_2019} discussed the benefit of part-time AAC use by speaking autistic adults, showing AAC can be beneficial to autistic adults and people who use speech. \citet{hanson_my_2014} showed that AAC can be beneficial as a supplemental strategy for people whose speech is not always intelligible. However, barriers like a lack of AAC knowledge and a belief that the ability to use speech means that AAC use is not acceptable can limit speaking autistic adults from using AAC in a supplemental manner.

\subsection{Technology Use by Autistic Adults}

Autistic people can use technology for a variety of communication and interaction needs.~\citet{das_towards_2021} explored how neurodivergent adults, including autistic adults, adapt to remote working environments using semi-structured interviews. These adaptations included managing attention and nonverbal communication during video meetings, exploring how to balance written and spoken communications, and maintaining mental health.~\citet{zolyomi_managing_2019} interviewed autistic adults to study how they use video calling for both work and personal communication. They found video calls could be stressful for autistic adults.~\citet{zolyomi_designing_2023} also studied how autistic and non-autistic people communicate with each other by finding common ground to guide their interactions and co-designing a way to visualize this common ground.

Incorporating technology of any kind into one's communication can be a difficult process. For example,~\citet{rackensperger_when_2005} conducted a focus group to explore how adults with cerebral palsy acquire and learn to use AAC. They found that there can be an upfront cost when acquiring and learning to use AAC prior to it becoming an effective mode of communication. \citet{pradana_dialogue_2025} explored the low adoption rate of AAC, noting that excluding AAC users from the design and development process contributes to this upfront cost. Pradana also found that AAC can be more than just a communication mode, such as expanding to become an integral component of social experiences. Both Rackensperger et al.~and Pradana state the importance of lived experience in conducting research. This importance is especially critical for autism research. \citet{morgado_ramirez_adult_2024} found in their review of the ACM Guide to Computing Literature that only 46 studies from 2007--2022 included autistic adults in their participant pool.

None of these studies explored how social skills, relationships, sensory preferences, or later-in-life diagnoses impacted autistic adults. More recent work has explored these topics with technology in general. For example, ~\citet{zolyomi_designing_2023} explored autistic and non-autistic pairs and how to design technology that supports finding common ground in their communication with each other. Other research has studied AAC specifically, such as~\citet{martin_bridging_2024} who conducted semi-structured interviews with autistic adults to explore their use of AAC. They discussed the impact of AAC content and its format (e.g., symbol- versus text-based content), safety when using AAC, and accessing AAC as an adult. More research is needed to explore how autistic adults use technology and AAC to communicate in social and community settings. Additionally, few studies make use of research methods beyond semi-structured interviews in studying autistic adults and their communication.

\section{Methods}

\subsection{Research Design}

We chose a focus group as the research methodology to gather qualitative data on the lived experiences of autistic adults and their experiences with social and community engagement \cite{krueger_focus_2015}. A focus group allows the participants to interact with each other, building up shared ideas and contrasting different lived experiences. Such data on compared life experiences cannot be achieved through interviews and surveys as these methodologies tend to focus on individuals. Autism presents differently in everyone, so each participant will have unique experiences that can also overlap with the experiences of the other participants. Using a focus group methodology allowed us to gather data on both the experiences of the individual participants and also how they compare and contrast their experiences to those of the other participants.

\subsection{Participants}

Participants met the following inclusion criteria: (a) be on the autism spectrum, (b) be 18 years of age or older, (c) be fluent in English, (d) be able to access the internet, use a web browser, and post messages in a forum. Recruitment was limited to the United States and was conducted through word-of-mouth and advertising to autism advocacy and support organizations.

~\citet{krueger_focus_2015} and~\citet{goodman_observing_2012} recommend 4--6 focus group participants in order for participants to feel comfortable and to collect more in-depth responses. As such, we recruited five participants for our focus group. We do not present per-participant demographics as several participants requested that their demographics only be reported in aggregate form. Participants ranged in age from 23--38 years old. Four identified as female and one identified as non-binary. The lack of male-identifying participants was not an intentional choice but simply reflects the gender identities of those who expressed interest in our study. Four participants self-disclosed receiving an autism diagnosis as a teenager (\textit{n}=1) or as an adult (\textit{n}=3). The remaining participant elected not to share their age of diagnosis. Participants used the following communication strategies: speech (\textit{n}=4), communication book (i.e., low-tech AAC)  (\textit{n}=1), speech-generating mobile applications (i.e., high-tech AAC)  (\textit{n}=2), and American Sign Language (\textit{n}=1).

\subsection{Focus group platform and structure}\label{platform_selection}

We conducted an online, asynchronous, text-based focus group using a forum software called Flarum.\footnote{\url{https://flarum.org/}} We hosted our Flarum server on the FreeFlarum service.\footnote{\url{https://freeflarum.com/}} Given that some of the participants were AAC users, we needed to make sure that the focus group was accessible to them. Previous research has used a text-based medium for focus groups with AAC users~\cite{caron_social_2017, dattilo_i_2008}. This allowed the focus group to be asynchronous, giving the participants as much time as they needed to process and compose messages. As discussed by~\citet{beneteau_who_2020}, asynchronous communication also opens research up to individuals who may not be otherwise able to participate in qualitative research.

We chose to make our focus group anonymous to protect the identities of our participants. Each was assigned a participant number in the form of P\# to be their alias for the focus group. We also wanted to limit the amount of direct contact participants could have with each other outside of the main focus group posts. We included the following Flarum plugins:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Approval
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Prevents participants from creating their own discussions but enables them to reply to discussions created by the admin.
    \end{itemize}
    \item Flags
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Allows users to flag posts for admin review.
        \item We instructed participants to flag any posts they thought were inappropriate. This feature was not used, but providing it was part of our strategy to protect participants.
    \end{itemize}
    \item Mentions
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Allows users to tag another user with @Username and cause a notification to that user (subject to the settings of the tagged user).
        \item The participants were told about this feature but did not use it, preferring to directly reply to other participants' posts to interact.
    \end{itemize}
    \item Subscriptions
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Allows users to follow discussions and receive notifications for new posts and replies in the discussion. \item While this feature was mentioned to the participants, they did not use it. We discuss this further in \cref{discussion}.
    \end{itemize}
    \item Suspend
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Allows admin to suspend users from posting, including posting replies.
        \item This feature was not used, but providing it was part of our strategy to protect the participants.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

Following guidance from \citet{krueger_focus_2015}, the first author acted as the moderator of the focus group and created a discussion board for each week of the study. We posted and pinned a post that contained the prompts for the participants to respond to at the start of each week, for example: ``Could you share some stories about how you engage with your community?'' 

Participants were required to respond to the weekly prompts and were also encouraged to respond to other participants' posts. We instructed participants to make their replies within the week but allowed participants to continue their discussions with each other even after the next week opened. Here is an overview of the focus group's weekly themes (see \cref{appendix_prompts} for the exact prompts):

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Week 1: Becoming familiar with the platform
    \item Week 2: Sharing stories about community engagement and the impact of their communication methods on that engagement
    \item Week 3: Sharing how environmental and interpersonal factors impact their communication
    \item Week 4: Sharing what they would like to see happen in the future
    \item Week 5: Providing feedback on the focus group methodology
\end{enumerate}

The research team reviewed all new posts across the discussion boards twice a day: once in the morning and once in the evening. Participants were sent a reminder email with a direct link to the week's discussion post three days before, one day before, and on the day a week's discussion replies were due.

\subsection{Analysis}

We used a two-stage analysis process on the participants' responses. To prepare for analysis, the first author read through all responses several times in what \citet{tracy_qualitative_2020} called the data immersion phase. Next, the first author developed first-cycle codes using the framework of~\citet{saldana_coding_2021} and applied these codes to phrases, sentences, and paragraphs using a single-coder approach. The first author then used pattern coding \cite{miles_qualitative_2020} on these first-cycle codes to create categories. The pattern coding process was then reapplied to these categories to create themes.

Following the process of \citet{creswell_qualitative_2023}, the second and third authors acted as peer reviewers, asking questions about the analysis method and interpretation throughout the analysis. The peer reviewers should be familiar with the research area and analysis process. This is done to ensure rigor in the qualitative analysis process \cite{creswell_qualitative_2023}. Additionally, the first author connected with each participant to explain the analysis process and our findings in a member reflection \cite{tracy_qualitative_2020}. During this member reflection, the first author presented the analysis process, analysis results, and the discussion section of this paper to each participant. The participant was then asked to provide feedback on whether they felt they were accurately represented in the results and discussion and to provide any other feedback.

