In almost every case (except for the 6-field scenario by the sampled model), close to 99\% of the predicted Lagrangians have less than 20\% errors. Despite only 7\% of training data being 6-field examples, the sampled model still handles these more complex cases remarkably well and comparable to the uniform model (trained with 16\% of training data containing six-field examples). This outcome is encouraging, as it demonstrates that focusing on shorter sequences during training can yield a model capable of effectively handling longer inputs without significantly compromising performance. Further improvement on the dataset would then require to balance the amount of long sequences and the amount of Lagrangians with trilinear terms, such that both a minimal priming rate is obtained and the model does not overestimate the number of terms.

