\section{Introduction}\label{sec:Introduction}
Dynamic graphs are ubiquitous where many real-world problems are dynamic in nature such as social networks \cite{pareja2020evolvegcn}, transportation networks \cite{cini2023scalable}, transaction networks \cite{shamsi2022chartalist}, and trade networks \cite{poursafaei2022towards}.
 Unlike static graphs, dynamic graphs evolve over time, with changes occurring in their topology or edge/node attributes. Message-passing-based graph representation learning models, such as Graph Neural Networks (GNN) and its variations \cite{kipf2016semi,velivckovic2017graph,ozdemir2024igcn,kesimoglu2023supreme,hamilton2017inductive}

have achieved significant success in graph analysis tasks. These models are effective in capturing local structural information due to the inherent locality of the message-passing mechanism. These models have recently been extended to dynamic graphs for representation learning \cite{you2022roland, zhu2023wingnn,wu2020temp,zhao2019t,li2024dynamic}. Many dynamic graph representation learning methods aim to effectively encode the temporal patterns for downstream tasks such as node classification \cite{li2024state,zhong2024efficient}, link prediction \cite{li2024dyg,alomranidyg2vec}, and recommendation \cite{li2019multi,he2023dynamically,zhang2022dynamic}. 

Some of dynamic prediction methods \cite{zhao2019t,bonner2019temporal,xue2021modeling},  process ``snapshot'' of the dynamic graph at each time point by employing a graph representation learning approach (e.g., Graph Convolutional Networks (GCN)).  
  Then to capture the temporal evolution of the graph over time, many methods utilize sequence encoders like Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) \cite{pareja2020evolvegcn} or Transformers \cite{sankar2020dysat}. 
   Although these approaches have demonstrated promising results, they have low expressive power as an independent GCN is trained for each snapshot of the graph. As a result, these models can not extract historical structural information. 

   Researchers have started addressing this issue by integrating the sequence encoder into the GCN layer to update the GCN model parameters \cite{pareja2020evolvegcn,sankar2020dysat}. However, these methods also have a limitation. In dynamic graphs, changes occurring in distant hops can influence the source node in subsequent snapshots, thus learning global features is crucial. However, GNN could only capture local information for each node as increasing number of hops to gather global information could lead to over-smoothing \cite{in2020graph}.  
   However, in dynamic graphs, changes occurring in distant hops can influence the source node in subsequent snapshots, which these methods fail to account for effectively. 
Meta-learning has emerged as an effective approach for modeling temporal dynamics. For example Oreshkin et al. \cite{oreshkin2002meta}  focused on the importance of meta-learning, as a transfer learning method, for successful zero-shot forecasting by designing a dual-loop structure that adapts quickly to new tasks while retaining knowledge from previous tasks. Recent research has leveraged meta-learning to reduce the number of model parameters  in dynamic graph. For instance, Yang et al. \cite{yang2022few} proposed a hierarchical adaptation method to update global parameters, You et al. \cite{you2022roland} utilized adjacent time snapshots to update embeddings and loss in a hybrid framework, and Zhu et al. \cite{zhu2023wingnn} employed a sliding window approach for updating model parameters. 
 While these methods represent the state-of-the-art (SOTA) in dynamic graph representation learning, they face a critical challenge. These meta-learning-based methods, such as WinGNN, update model parameters using a sliding window without considering the model's performance or leveraging information from earlier snapshots during updates. For example, WinGNN typically adjust parameters based on the gradient of the loss in the adjacent snapshot. However, if the model performs poorly on that snapshot, the update might lead to suboptimal results. This process lacks a mechanism for "forgetting" less relevant snapshots and "remembering" critical patterns from earlier ones. 
  In dynamic graphs, patterns can occur across consecutive snapshots, and failing to account for this temporal continuity limits the effectiveness of these models. Thus, a model that can capture time-wise changes is important.

To address the aforementioned challenges, we introduce a method called Multi-view Dynamic Graph Embeddings with State Space Model Gradient Update (DyGSSM). DyGSSM integrates both local and features of a graph using cross attention.  
  We utilized SSM to update model parameters. 
  We computed the gradient of the loss in the current snapshot, weighted by the model performance on the current snapshot and pass it to the next snapshot. 
  The SSM   considers the loss from each snapshot during parameter updates, which facilitates smoother updates and more efficient capture of model dependencies across time. Inspired from \cite{zhu2023wingnn}, we updated model parameters within a overlapping window of snapshots. 

  We summarize the main contributions of this study as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item We introduced a global and local view of the dynamic graph that captures both local and global changes for each source node. This approach allows the model to have a broader perspective on each node. To integrate the embeddings from the local and global views, we employ a cross-attention-based method, ensuring a seamless fusion of both types of information. 
    \item We introduced a SSM-based method using HiPPO algorithm to effectively capture long-term dependencies when updating model parameters. Unlike traditional approaches, our model evaluates its performance in each snapshot before performing any parameter updates. 
    \item Extensive experiments on five public datasets demonstrate the superiority of DyGSSM compared to SOTA models.
\end{itemize}




