\section{Related Work}\label{sec:RelatedWorks}
 Many dynamic graph representation can broadly be categorized into three groups. \textbf{Sequence-based models:} These studies adopt a two-stage representation learning approach. They combine message-passing models with sequence encoders to capture temporal dependencies effectively. \textbf{Meta-learner-based models:} This group extends static graph methods to dynamic graphs by employing gradient-based meta-learning strategies to build prediction models for dynamic data. \textbf{Transformer and State Space Model (SSM)-based models:} These methods leverage the capabilities of Transformers and SSMs to model dynamic graphs, taking advantage of their strengths in handling sequential and temporal data efficiently.



\subsection{Dynamic Graph Sequence-Based Models}\label{sec:SequenceBased}
Sequence based models are those that use a message-passing framework at each snapshot, and subsequently a sequence encoder model on the output \cite{wu2020temp}. For example, Manessi et al. \cite{manessi2020dynamic} proposed a novel method by combining GCN and LSTM. They applied GCN to obtain the embeddings of each snapshot and passed the embedding to LSTM for time sequence dependencies. Manessi et al. \cite{manessi2020dynamic} also employed GCN and LSTM in two settings  for classification sequence of graph and vertices. Pareja et al. \cite{pareja2020evolvegcn} combined GCN and GRU for link prediction, edge classification, and node classification.  In \cite{cini2023scalable}, the authors propose a method that embeds the embedding history into higher dimensional state vectors using randomized Recurrent Neural Networks. Jiang et al. \cite{jiang2022graph} and Liu et al. \cite{liu2024rt} also combine GCN and GRU for forecasting based on spatiotemporal analysis and traffic prediction. Hu et al. \cite{hu2021time} integrate graph-level propagation with GNN and RNN
components to capture temporal graph information in dynamic graph. In their settings, they consider each node as a state and each edge as the transitional relations across adjacent segments. The GNN extract the local-pairwise
relations among nodes in each graph, while RNN capture temporal influence and how relations evolve over different graphs. 

Transformer is one of the recent breakthroughs in Natural Language processing that applies RNN-based models. To that end, many researchers have used Transformer instead of LSTM or GRU to capture this dependency. For example, Sankar et al. \cite{sankar2020dysat} employs self-attention in two different aspects. First, attend to structural neighborhoods at each time point. Second, attend to previous historical representations to conduct link prediction. Their model can be considered as a variant of Graph Attention Networks. Jin et al. \cite{jin2023spatio} combines GCN and transformer to jointly capture the spatio-temporal dependencies for predicting traffic congestion time. Wang et al.  \cite{wang2023networked} introduced an encoder-decoder architecture for dynamic graph representation. They utilize a Random Walk with Restart approach which enables the model to be more expressive than GNN message-passing frameworks. In addition, they used the concept of the sliding window with a Lagrange multiplier to control the amount of information that can be transmitted through the latent representation.

