\subsection{Dynamic Graph Meta-Learner-Based Models}\label{sec:MetaLearnerBased}
Meta-learning is based on the idea of transfer learning, where previous experience is used to quickly adapt to a new task. Dynamic graph meta-learner based models are those that extend static graph models to dynamic data using previous time gradient aggregation. The purpose of using meta-learning in GNN was to transfer the label information to unlabeled sample through massage passing \cite{satorras2018few}. Zhou et al. \cite{zhou2019meta} leveraged the meta-leaning approach to learn better model parameter initialization of GNN for a few shot node classification task. Their proposed model can be adopted to new task and classes. Hwang et al. \cite{hwang2021self} proposed a new self-supervised auxiliary learning framework using meta-learning approach to leverage rich information of heterogeneous graphs. They applied their models on link prediction and node classification tasks and reported that the meta-learning approach improve their performance.  
Ma et al. \cite{ma2020adaptive} proposed a GNN-based graph meta-learner model that can effectively capture sub-structures on unseen graphs. Inspired by Reinforcement learning, they designed a controller for meta-learner that provide optimal adaption steps. 

You et al. \cite{you2022roland} proposed a hybrid network that propagates learned embeddings and loss from the current snapshot to the next. Their model efficiently extends static graphs to dynamic ones with minimal extra computational cost. They use a two-layer GNN, where each layer updates its parameters and passes them to the adjacent snapshot. Another approach taken by Zhu et al. \cite{zhu2023wingnn} designed a dynamic GNN using a meta-learning strategy. Their approach employs a sliding-window gradient-aggregation function to pass loss gradients. Instead of traditional encoders, they use random sliding windows and adaptive gradients, reducing model parameters. Their sliding-window mechanism captures longer dependencies beyond just adjacent snapshots.
%You et al. \cite{you2022roland}, the authors proposed a hybrid network that can forward the learned embedding and loss of the current snapshot to the next (adjacent) snapshots. Their model can easily extend static graphs to dynamic ones without much extra computational cost. In their settings, they have two layer GNN and each layer forwards its parameters update to the adjacent snapshot. Another approach taken by Zhu et al. \cite{zhu2023wingnn}, is to attempt to create a dynamic GNN by employing a meta-learning strategy and passing the gradient of the loss function using a sliding-window gradient-aggregation function. They replace the encoders with random sliding windows and adaptive gradient that lead to model parameter reduction. The slidig window designed to extract longer dependency than adjacent snapshot. 
  Yang et al. \cite{yang2022few}  leverage a meta-learning strategy for few-shot link prediction in dynamic graphs. They introduce time interval-wise and node-wise adaptions to encompass time dependency and node dependency features and update the global parameter. 

\subsection{Dynamic Graph SSM- and Transformers-based methods}
Graph Transformer (GT) gained popularity in the field of graph representation. 
%The graph community has recently started to embrace transformer on graph. %For example Chen et al. \cite{chen2022structure} proposed a structure aware transformer. They n incorporates structural information into the original self attention. They showed that transformers with positional encoding do not necessarily capture structural properties. Therefore, they utilized GNNs to obtain initial node representations. Transformer has been applied to dynamic graph as well. 
 For example,  Ye et al. \cite{ye2022meta} proposed to use transformer on spatial temporal traffic prediction. Their framework is a generalization of the original transformer where encoder task is to encode historical traffic data into intermediate representations, and decoder performs future traffic prediction. Varghese et al. \cite{varghese2024transformerg2g} utilized transformer for learning temporal graph. They used only transformer encoder to  learn intermediate node representations from all the previous states up to current state. They used two projection heads (linear mapping and non-linear mapping) to generate low dimension  latent embedding at different snapshots. Biparva et al. \cite{biparva2024todyformer} utilized transformer to unify  local and global message-passing. They introduced patchifying, tokenization, and encoding modules to prevent over-squashing and over-smoothing. Yu et al. \cite{yu2023towards} proposed a simple transformer based model that uses nodes\ historical first-hop interactions by having  neighbor co-occurrence encoding methods to find the correlation of two nodes based on the historical sequence.  Chen et al. \cite{chen2024dtformer} utilized transformer encoder for dynamic graph representation learning. First, they collect all the first-hop neighbors of source and destination nodes (which they aim to do link prediction). Then, they map these neighbor features into sequence to be processed by transformer.  

 Despite the effectiveness of transformer on graph, it suffers from having  quadratic computational cost and lack of  inductive biases on graph structures. Recent success of SSM-based models (such as Mamba) in Computer Vision (CV) and Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks motivate researchers to apply SSM based model on graphs. For example Ding et al. \cite{ding2024dygmamba} introduced two levels of SSM, a node-level SSM to encode node interaction and a time-level SSM to exploit the temporal patterns. Yuan et al.  \cite{yuan2024dg} proposed a dynamic graph learning using Mamba. They consider a dynamic graph as a self-contained system with SSM to capture  global intrinsic dynamics. They discretized the system state and implied adjacency relationships between nodes across different snapshots. Li et al. \cite{li2024dyg} proposed a new continuous SSM for dynamic graph learning. They consider  irregular time spans
as control signals for SSM to have robust and generalizable model. They also introduced a linear cross-attention layer on top
of the DyG-Mamba layer to improve the model performance. 
