\section{Problem Formulation}
Let $\V$ be a set of nodes and $\E$ be the set of edges that connects the nodes in $\V$. 
%Nodes that are connected by an edge are represented by pairs $(v_i, v_j)$ for $1\leq i,j \leq n$, and the collection of edges is contained in the set $\E$. 
A graph consists of three components $\G = (\V, \E, \X)$, where $\X\in\R^{n\times m}$ is a feature matrix where $n=|\V|$ and $m$ is the feature dimension size. For a graph $\G$, we can create an adjacency matrix $A \in \R^{n\times n}$ where 
\beq 
A_{ij} = \left\{
\ba{ll}
1 & \mbox{ if } (v_i, v_j) \in \E, \\
0 & \mbox{ otherwise. }
\ea
\right.
\eeq
To study temporal data, we let $G =\{\G_1,\dots,\G_T\}$ be a sequence of graphs for discrete snapshots $t =1, \dots, T$. Here, each $\G_t = (\V_t , \E_t , \X_t )$ represents a snapshot of the dynamic graph with adjacency matrix $A_t$ at time $t$. 

To evaluate DyGSSM, we consider a link prediction task in which our model has to learn a binary classification function to predict whether two nodes are connected at time $t + 1$ given their information in time $t$. Specifically, as will be explained later, let $\X_t^{local}$, and $\X_t^{global}$ represent local and global node embeddings in $\G_t$, then the binary classification function is defined as follows:
% \beq\label{eq:interpretationModel}
%     \mathcal{F}\left( \X^{local}, \X^{global},  \X^{fused} \right) \rightarrow \mathcal{F}
% \eeq


\beq\label{eq:interpretationModel}
\begin{split}
\X^{fused}_t \leftarrow CA(\X^{local}_t, \X^{global}_t)\\
    \mathcal{F}\left(\G_{t}, \X^{fused}_t \right) \rightarrow \E_{t+1}
\end{split}
\eeq
where $CA$ is the cross-attention function to fuse the embeddings and $\mathcal{F}$ is the prediction function.
%$\E_{t+1}$ is a edge between nodes in the next snapshots. 
