\section{Method}\label{sec:Methods}
In this section, we describe the architecture of DyGSSM shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:enter-arc}. First, we will discuss the procedure for capturing inner-time node embeddings, i.e., processing features of the static graph at each snapshot. We first give an overview of SSM in Subsection~\ref{Preliminaries}. In Subsection~\ref{sec:GAT}, we describe the graph convolution method to obtain $\X^{local}$. Next, in Subsection~\ref{sec:GRU}, we describe the biased random walk coupled with GRU to compute $\X^{global}$. In Section~\ref{sec:CA}, we describe the cross-attention-based method to compute $\X^{fused}$ by integrating $\X^{local}$ and $\X^{global}$.  Finally, we describe SSM-based meta-learning update in Subsection~\ref{snapshotoptim}.


\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{Preliminaries}
A State Space Model (SSM) frames a discrete-time state-space representation by a linear mapping from a discrete input  $u_t$ at time $t$ to a discrete output $y_t$ through a state variable $s_t$ and $K$, $G$ and $C$ matrices as follows:

\beq\label{eq:hippo1}
    s_t = K s_{t - 1} + Gu_t 
\eeq
\beq\label{eq:hippo2}
    y_t = C s_t + D u_t
\eeq
The structure of matrix $K$ is important when building an SSM, as this matrix determines which part of the previous state can be passed to the current state. Many language models that are based on SSM initialize the matrix $K$ using High-order Polynomial Projection Operators (HiPPO) \cite{gu2022train,gu2021efficiently}. Using HiPPO to initialize the matrix $K$ was shown to perform better than initializing it as a random matrix. The  HiPPO matrix is designed to generate a hidden state that can memorize the past inputs. In Section~\ref{snapshotoptim}, we show how we utilized the HiPPO algorithm to define our $K$ matrix to handle long-range dependencies.



% has  been used to preserve the sequence history by projecting it on a basis of orthogonal polynomials \cite{gu2020hippo,fu2022hungry}, that translate K and G matrices in Equation~\ref{eq:hippo1}  to some special matrices.

\subsection{Inner-time Local Node Embeddings}\label{sec:GAT}

% In this section, we start by modeling local and global node embeddings of each node of each graph at a specific time. We then explain our proposed meta-learning parameter updates using HIPPO algorithm. 
% \subsubsection{Graph Neural Network}
The objective of this step is to learn local embeddings of nodes of a graph snapshot using GNN. Graph representation techniques, such as GCN update the embedding of each node $u$ in $\G_t$ (i.e., snapshot of a dynamic graph at time $t$) by performing massage passing between neighbor nodes as follows:

\beq\label{eq:gcn}
h^{(l)}_{t,u} = UPDATE(h^{(l - 1)}_{t,u} , AGG({h^{(l - 1)}_{t,v}, \forall v \in \mathcal{N}_{t, u}})),
\eeq

where $h^{(l)}_{t,u}$, the embedding of node $u$ at layer $l$, is computed by aggregating information from all its neighbors at that time $\mathcal{N}_{t, u}$. To update all nodes, this process is defined in matrix format as ${H^{(l)}_t  = \sigma (\hat{A}_t H^{(l - 1)}_t W^{(l)}_t)}$ where $H^{(l)}_t\in\R^{n \times d}$ is the embedding of nodes in $\G_t$ derived at the l-th layer, $\hat{A}_t = D^{-1/2}_t A_t D^{-1/2}_t$ is the normalized adjacent matrix, where $A_t$ is the adjacency matrix of $\G_t$ and $D^{-1/2}_t$ is the diagonal degree matrix of $\G_t$, $W^{(l)}_t$ is the learnable parameter matrix at l-th layer at time $t$, and $\sigma$ represents a non-linear activation function.  Following WinGNN, we update $H^{(l)}_t$ as follows:

\beq\label{eq:layerupdate}
\begin{split}
    \hat{H}^{(l)}_t = H^{(l)}_t  O^{(l)} + \beta_O^{(l)} \\
    SC = H^{(l - 1)}_t  J^{(l)} + \beta_J^{(l)} \\
    \hat{H}^{(l)}_t = \hat{H}^{(l)}_t + SC
\end{split}
\eeq

where $O^{(l)}$ and $J^{(l)}$ are two trainable weight matrices that are shared across snapshots and belong to the layer $l$, with their corresponding biased terms, $\beta_O^{(l)}$ and $\beta_J^{(l)}$, respectively. $SC$ stands for skip connection in Equation \ref{eq:layerupdate}. In this study, we used two-layer message passing to compute local node embeddings. For convenience  and consistency, we represent the final local node embedding ( $\hat{H}^{(l)}_t$) as $\X^{local}_t$. 
