\subsubsection{Traditional GRU architecture}
After computing the RW, we passed the generated sequence $x_t$ to a GRU model as formulated below: %and similar to \cite{a6}.
\beq\label{eq:gru}
\begin{split}
    z^s = \sigma \left(W^z \cdot [h^{s - 1}, x_t] + b^z \right) \\ 
    r^s = \sigma \left(W^r \cdot [h^{s - 1}, x_t] + b^r \right) \\
    \hat{h}^s = tanh \left(W^h \cdot [r^{s} \times h^{s - 1}, x_t] + b^h \right) \\
       h^s =   \left(1 - z^s \right) \times  h^{s - 1} + z^s \times \hat{h}^{s}
   \end{split}
%\end{split}
\eeq
where $h^s$ is the hidden state of layer 1. We used a two-layer GRU by passing $h^s$ as input instead of $x_t$ in Equation~\ref{eq:gru}. All the weights in both layers with their corresponding biases
are shared across snapshots. For convenience and consistency, we represent the final global node embedding of all nodes in $\G_t$ as $\X_t^{global}$.  
%The final embeddings for snapshot t, is $X_t^{global}$. 

\subsubsection{Light GRU architecture}
Feng et al. \cite{feng2024were} introduced a lighter version of GRU and LSTM, which has fewer parameters than their traditional counterparts, are fully parallelizable during training, and achieve competitive performance on a range of tasks. They simplify GRU in two steps: first, removing the dependency of previous hidden state, second, removing the tanh function on the  hidden states and change Equation \ref{eq:gru} as follow:


\beq\label{eq:gru1}
\begin{split}
    z^s = \sigma \left(W^z \cdot x_t + b^z \right) \\ 
    \hat{h}^s = \left(W^h \cdot  x_t + b^r \right) \\
   h^s =   \left(1 - z^s \right) \times  h^{s - 1} + z^s \times \hat{h}^{s} \\
   \end{split}
\eeq

%Obviously Equation \ref{eq:gru1} has less number of parameters in compare to to Equation \ref{eq:gru}. 
