\subsection{Integration of
Local and Global Node Embeddings}\label{sec:CA}
%Although these two embeddings belong to the same time steps, the underling embeddings representation is different. To integrate $\X^{local}_t$ and $\X^{global}_t$ embeddings, we adopt a multi-head cross-attention mechanism. Note that we following \re{Facebook paper} and defined two cross attentions where one time we consider $\X^{local}_t$ as query, $\X^{global}_t$ as key and value, and one time $\X^{global}_t$ as query $\X^{local}_t$ as key and value, as presented in \ref{fig:crossattention}. However, as presented in ablation study, the double cross attention was performing worser than single cross attention. To that reason we consider the single cross attention in this study. The cross attention $CA$ is calculated as follows: 
%\textcolor{red}{Please make sure to use past tense consistently for your methods. check everywhere carefully.}
Although the two embeddings, $\X^{local}_t$ and $\X^{global}_t$, belong to the same snapshot, their underlying representations are distinct. To integrate these embeddings, we adopted a cross-attention mechanism. %Following the approach outlined in \cite{kirillov2023segment}, we define two cross-attention operations: one where $\X^{local}_t$ serves as the query, and $\X^{global}_t$ serves as both the key and value, and another where $\X^{global}_t$ serves as query, with $\X^{local}_t$ as the key and value. However, as demonstrated in our ablation study, the dual cross-attention mechanism performed worse than using a single cross-attention mechanism. Therefore, in this study, we employ only the single cross-attention mechanism. 
 We considered $\X^{local}$ as our query because the GCN model was the one whose parameters were updated across time. %Therefore, that is what we focused on. 
 The cross-attention $CA$ is calculated as follows:

\beq\label{eq:integration}
     CA(\mathcal{Q}_t^{local}, \mathcal{K}_t^{global}, \mathcal{V}_t^{global}) = \softmax\left(\frac{\mathcal{Q}_t^{local} \mathcal{K}_t^{global}}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right) \mathcal{V}_t^{global}
\eeq
%Here, $\mathcal{Q}_t$, $\mathcal{K}_t$, and $\mathcal{V}_t$ are tensors and are the linear transformation of the input,

where $Q^{local}_t = W_q*\X^{local}_t$, $K^{global}_t = W_k*\X^{global}_t$, $V^{global}_t = W_v*\X^{global}_t$. We represent this fused embedding as $X_t^{fused}$.

Note that $W_q$, $W_k$, and $W_v$ were shared across times. We used a single-head attention to keep the parameters reasonable. %In the case of multi-head attention, the shape of Q, V, K is (heads*nodes*embeddings) $\rightarrow$ 4 heads, 100 nodes, 300 dimensions (4, 100, 300). 
 %\sout{This is for single-head attention, but if we do the same process $h$ times, where $h$ represents the number of heads, followed by concatenating multi-head, linear mapping, layer normalization, and dropout, then we obtain multi-head attention embedding} \textcolor{red}{in your code did you use multi-head attention or single head. it's more important to describe what you implemented. also specify number of heads}. % 1 and 2. We integrate multi-head cross attention embeddings as follows:

% \begin{equation} \label{eq:integration}
%     \X^{\CA} = \omega \, \crossattention_1 + (1-\omega) \, \crossattention_2
% \end{equation}
% where $\omega\in[0,1]$. 
Equation~\ref{eq:integration} acts as a gate that controls the amount of information that $\X^{local}_t$ and $\X^{global}_t$ can transmit to the final representation of the graph at time $t$. As mentioned in Section~\ref{sec:Introduction}, we used a window size to update the model parameters. Below we first talk about snapshot-wise update and then talk about the window size update. 

