\subsection{Integration and Global Loss Calculation in Each Snapshot}\label{losses}
To perform gradient optimization in a single snapshot, we defined a binary cross entropy loss  function, as in this study, we worked on link prediction tasks. We utilized and calculated the loss of model in each snapshot as:
\beq    \label{eq:loss}
\mathcal{L}_t^{fused} = - \frac{1}{M} \sum_{(u, v) \in \E_t}^M y^t_{u, v} \cdot log (\hat{y}^t_{u, v}) + (1 - y^t_{u, v}) \cdot log(1 - \hat{y}^t_{u, v})
\eeq
where $ \hat{y}^t_{u, v}$ is calculated as a dot product $\hat{y}^t_{u, v} = h^{fused}_{u,t} \cdot (h^{fused}_{v,t})^T$. $h^{fused}_{u,t}$ is the final hidden embeddings of node $u$ at time $t$ after we perform cross attention between global and local information of node $u$.  Although global information is part of the loss, we only updated cross attention and GCN parameters when we  do backpropagation using above loss. To update GRU parameters we follow the same loss as follows:

\beq    \label{eq:loss}
\mathcal{L}_t^{global} = - \frac{1}{M} \sum_{(u, v) \in \E_t}^M y^t_{u, v} \cdot log (\hat{{\hat  y}}^t_{u, v}) + (1 - y^t_{u, v}) \cdot log(1 - \hat{{\hat  y}}^t_{u, v})
\eeq
where $\hat{{\hat  y}}^t_{u, v}$ is calculated as 
    $\hat{{\hat  y}}^t_{u, v} = h^{s}_{u,t} \cdot (h^{s}_{v,t})^T$ and  $h^{s}_{u,t}$ is the global embedding of node $u$ at time $t$. 

