\subsection{Gradient Optimization in a Single Snapshot}\label{snapshotoptim}
The main objective of this step is to calculate the gradient of the current snapshot, efficiently optimize model parameters, and propagate it to the next snapshot. State Space Model (SSM) proven to be an efficient approach for capturing both short-term and long-term dependencies.  Compared to sequence-based models such as GRU and LSTM, SSM offers improved memory efficiency and requires fewer parameters.


% \textbf{start} To preserve the sequence history, HiPPO [24]
% projects the history on a basis of orthogonal polynomials, which translates to having SSMs whose A, B
% matrices are initialized to some special matrices.\textbf{end} 

 After calculating $\mathcal{L}_t^{fused}$ at time $t$, we employ a simple dynamic weighting mechanism to adjust the influence of each snapshot during parameter updates as presented bellow:
\begin{equation}\label{eq9}
    weight_t = \frac{1}{\mathcal{L}_t^{fused} + \epsilon} 
\end{equation}

Equation \ref{eq9} can be considered as a gate, determining the extent to which information from past states influences the current state. This approach ensures a more balanced flow of information across snapshots, enabling the model to adapt to temporal dynamics while maintaining consistency in parameter updates.

