Specifically, if the model's loss at a given snapshot is high, the dynamic weight reduces the impact of that particular state on the parameter update. This approach ensures that snapshots with higher losses contribute less to the model's learning process.  


To incorporate the SSM in our gradient update in each snapshot, we considered Equation \ref{eq:hippo1}, and slightly changed it to get 
    ${s_{t}=\hat{K} s_{t -1}+\hat{G}_t weight_t}$ where $s_{t - 1}$ is the state vector at time $t - 1$, where we initialized as a zero vector for $t=0$. $\hat{G}_t$ is the gradient vector of model parameter with respect to loss at time $t$ and $\hat{K}$ is the projection matrix. We initialized $\hat{K}$ using the HiPPO algorithm \cite{gu2022train,gu2021efficiently}. 

\begin{equation}
\hat{K}_{i, j} =
    \begin{cases}
      (-1) ^{i-j} (2i + 1) & \text{if i > j}\\
      2 & \text{if i= j}\\
      0 & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases} 
\end{equation}


This step-by-step processing allows the SSM   to capture and incorporate the evolving dynamics of the graph over time effectively. 
For entries where the row index is greater than the column index ($i> j$), the equation above fills the values along the lower diagonal. $(2i +1)$ acts as linear function of the row index $i$ and takes a positive or negative sign depending on whether $i - j$ in $(-1)^{i - j}$ is even or odd. If $i - j$ is even, the output is positive; otherwise, it is negative. On the diagonal ($i= j$), this matrix has a value of 2, and above the diagonal ($i< j$), the values are set to 0.
