\subsection{Sliding window-based Multi-snapshot Gradient Optimization}
The main objective of this section is to perform window-based  multi-snapshot gradient optimization. Many previous meta-learning-based approaches, such as \cite{you2022roland}, update parameters using only adjacent snapshots. However, this type of model parameter update relies solely on information from adjacent snapshots and cannot capture long-range dependencies. Window-based  multi-snapshot gradient optimization has been proven to be an effective approach for capturing longer dependencies \cite{zhu2023wingnn}.
Unlike in \cite{zhu2023wingnn} where a randomized sliding-window was used, we processed snapshots sequentially to mimic the natural temporal flow of information. 
We considered an overlapped sliding windows and every time we shifted it by one to right. The window size can be represented by $\Delta t$, where, for example, if  $\Delta t= 8$, then the model considers ($\G_0$, $\G_1$, .., $\G_7$). For each snapshot, for example $\G_1$, we calculate the loss and update the model parameters, as explained in Section~\ref{losses}. This can refer to snapshot $\G_{t - 1}$ in Figure \ref{fig:enter-arc} (black arrow).  Then, the next snapshot loss is calculated to perform multi-snapshot gradient back propagation.  This can refer to snapshot $\G_{t}$ in Figure \ref{fig:enter-arc} (red arrow). Thus we have two final loss as follows:

% \beq\label{eq:windowloss}
% \begin{split}
%     \hat{\mathcal{L}}_{G_{t:\Delta t}} = (\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{G_{t + 1}}, \dots \hat{\mathcal{L}}_{G_{\Delta t - 1}})  \\ 
%    \hat{\mathcal{L}}_{GRU_{t:\Delta t}} = (\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{GRU_{t + 1}}, \dots \hat{\mathcal{L}}_{GRU_{\Delta t - 1}})
% \end{split}
% \eeq


\beq\label{eq:finalloss}
\begin{split}
    \mathcal{L}_{fused} = \frac{1}{\Delta t} \sum_{t=1}^{\Delta t} \hat{\mathcal{L}}_{t}^{fused}  \\ 
    \mathcal{L}_{global} = \frac{1}{\Delta t} \sum_{t=0}^{\Delta t} \hat{\mathcal{L}}_{t}^{fused}
\end{split}
\eeq

A detailed description of training process can be
found in Algorithm 1.

