\subsection{Ablation study}
To evaluate the contribution of each component in DyGSSM, we performed an ablation study utilizing the UCI-Message dataset. %We conducted the ablation study on UCI message passing dataset only, due to time \sout{ and memory constraints}. 
Specifically, we analyzed the performance of DyGSSM when using double cross-attention instead of single cross attention, replacing GRU with LSTM, performing random window scanning (similar to WinGNN), disabling cross-attention when integrating local and global embeddings, disabling global embeddings, and disabling SSM. The results indicate that all key components contribute to DyGSSM’s performance (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}). DyGSSM obtained the best performance in all evaluation metrics with one exception. When we disabled the SSM, the model performed slightly better in terms of AUC, but DyGSSM outperformed for other metrics with a large margin. The poor performance of DyGSSM with a random window size indicates that randomness in the window size severely affects DyGSSM's ability to capture long-term dependencies. Although DyGSSM without cross-attention and without global information perform similarly in terms of Accuracy and AUC, they show a significant gap in Recall@10 and MRR. In DyGSSM without cross-attention, we used a simple summation operation. However, when there is no global information, cross-attention is automatically disabled. This suggests that using summation to fuse global and local information leads to poor results, indicating the importance of cross-attention. Note that DyGSSM without SSM results in worse MRR and Recall@10 performance compared to DyGSSM without global information. This also indicates the importance of SSM in our update process.
