\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
In this study, we propose DyGSSM, a multi-view dynamic graph representation learning approach for link prediction tasks. We trained DyGSSM in a supervised manner, leveraging both the local and global structure of each node in each snapshot to generate two distinct node embeddings. We then used a single-head cross-attention mechanism to integrate these embeddings. To effectively incorporate past information when updating the model parameters, we utilized an SSM-based approach using the HiPPO algorithm. Following recent dynamic graph representation techniques, we incorporated a meta-learning strategy into the SSM   to compute the gradient of the loss in the current snapshot. We then weighted the SSM matrix by the model loss in that snapshot before passing the updated model parameters to the next snapshot. Experiments on five public datasets with varying densities show that DyGSSM or Light\_DyGSSM outperforms SOTA models on 17 out of 20 metrics. As a future work, we plan to apply DyGSSM to continuous-time dynamic graphs to evaluate its performance in that setting.% In this study, we proposed a multi-view  dynamic graphs representation learning for link prediction tasks. We trained the model in a supervised manner. We leveraged both local and global structure of each node in each snapshot to generate two different node embeddings. We then used a single head cross attention to integrate these two embeddings. To effectively incorporate past information when updating the model parameters, we used SSM   based oh HIPPO algorithm. We followed recent dynamic graph representation techniques and incorporate a meta-learning strategy to SSM   to compute
% the gradient of the loss in the current snapshot. We then weight the SSM matrix by model loss in that snapshot before passing the updated model parameters to the next snapshot. Experiment on 5 public datset with different density showed that, DyGSSM outperform SOTA model on 18 out of 20 metrics. 

