\begin{abstract}
% With the rapid development of collaborative robotics, autonomous driving, and extended reality, point cloud (PC) transmission has garnered increasing attention from both academia and industry. However, the massive volume of PC data presents significant challenges for efficient transmission. Although semantic communication (SemCom), known for its high compression ratio, appears to be a promising solution, the unstructured and unordered nature of point clouds hinders its development, leaving SemCom for PC still in its early stages. To this end, we propose a novel channel-adaptive cross-modal generative SemCom for PC transmission, called GenSeC-PC. Specifically, we develop a semantic encoder that leverages two modalities of data—images and PCs—where the images serve as side information to aid in more precise semantic extraction but are not transmitted to the receiver. To ensure robust transmission of semantic features, we design a channel-adaptive joint semantic-channel coding architecture that accounts for both signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and available bandwidth, enabling a balanced trade-off between model redundancy and reconstruction accuracy. At the receiver side, a diffusion-based semantic decoder is employed. Moreover, we integrate rectified diffusion into the decoding process to accelerate decoding, thereby enabling real-time PC transmission. Moreover, the generative prior learning of the decoder brings two notable advantages to the proposed GenSeC-PC. Firstly, it enables reliable reconstruction even when the source point cloud is noisy or incomplete. Moreover, it supports fully analog transmission. This significantly improves compression efficiency compared to existing SemCom for PCs, which depend on error-free transmission of essential information. The simulation results validate the superiority of cross-modal semantic extraction and demonstrate the framework's robustness under both low SNR  and limited bandwidth conditions, while achieving an excellent trade-off between real-time performance and accuracy in the decoding process.
With the rapid development of autonomous driving and extended reality, efficient transmission of point clouds (PCs) has become increasingly important.  In this context, we propose a novel channel-adaptive cross-modal generative semantic communication (SemCom) for PC transmission, called GenSeC-PC. GenSeC-PC employs a semantic encoder that fuses images and point clouds, where images serve as \textit{non-transmitted} side information. Meanwhile, the decoder is built upon the backbone of PointDif. Such a cross-modal design not only ensures high compression efficiency but also delivers superior reconstruction performance compared to PointDif. Moreover, to ensure robust transmission and reduce system complexity, we design a streamlined and asymmetric  channel-adaptive joint semantic-channel coding architecture, where only the encoder needs the feedback of average signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and available bandwidth. In addition, rectified denoising diffusion implicit models is employed to accelerate the decoding process to the millisecond level, enabling real-time PC communication. Unlike existing methods, GenSeC-PC leverages generative priors to ensure reliable reconstruction even from noisy or incomplete source PCs. More importantly, it supports \textit{fully analog} transmission, improving compression efficiency by eliminating the need for error-free side information transmission common in prior SemCom approaches. Simulation results confirm the effectiveness of cross-modal semantic extraction and dual-metric guided fine-tuning, highlighting the framework’s robustness across diverse conditions—including low SNR, bandwidth limitations, varying numbers of 2D images, and previously unseen objects.
\end{abstract}



