Figure~\ref{fig: visualexample2} compares the transmission distortion of GenSeC-PC and a conventional communication method based on Octree encoding (depth = 8) under different channel conditions. As shown in the figure, even a single bit error in the conventional method can lead to unpredictable and severe deformation of the PC shape. In contrast, GenSeC-PC consistently preserves the overall shape across various channel conditions. This demonstrates the remarkable robustness of GenSeC-PC against transmission-induced errors.
Subsequently, we conduct a comprehensive comparison between GenSeC-PC and conventional methods under various settings, in terms of point cloud reconstruction quality, bandwidth usage, and the number of generated points. To ensure reliable transmission, we employ a rate-1/2 LDPC channel code together with BPSK or QPSK modulation. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:CC}(a), the conventional method exhibits a clear ``cliff effect".  When the Octree encoding depth is set to 8, decoding fails entirely under low-SNR conditions. Although reducing the depth to 4 enables successful decoding, the resulting distortion remains extremely high. In contrast, the proposed GenSeC-PC achieves significantly lower CD across various SNR and rate settings.
Furthermore, Fig.~\ref{fig:CC}(b) illustrates the number of transmitted symbols, where GenSeC-PC requires substantially less bandwidth—over 1000× fewer symbols in some cases. It is also worth noting that although reducing the Octree depth reduces the transmitted code size, it comes at the cost of significantly degraded reconstruction accuracy. Even under lossless transmission, Octree decoding still results in higher CD compared to GenSeC-PC, likely due to insufficient point density caused by the complexity of the object shape as conveyed by Fig.~\ref{fig:CC}(c).


% We consider the traditional transmission pipeline using Octree-based source coding, which is a widely recognized and commonly adopted approach for compressing and encoding PCs, as the baseline algorithm. To ensure reliable transmission, we employ a rate-1/4 LDPC channel code together with BPSK modulation. Without loss of generality, we only consider the CD metric in this part. The performance comparison between GenSeC-PC and the traditional method under different SNR levels and bit error rates (BERs) is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:CC}. Under each channel condition, 50 independent transmissions are performed, and the decoding failure rate is recorded. The CD mean is calculated by averaging only the successful decoding instances. Meanwhile, some visual results are presented in Fig.~\ref{fig: visualexample2}. Firstly, for the performance of traditional method, we can see that when the SNR is relatively low (below 5 dB), there is nearly a 10\% probability of decoding failure. Moreover, at an SNR of 0 dB and a BER level of 0.01, even when decoding is successful, the reconstructed shape may still suffer significant distortion. In contrast to GenSeC-PC, the traditional method tends to generate more than ten times the number of points, with these points clustered very closely together. As shown in the figure, the points essentially collapse to a single point, with no useful information retained. When the SNR is 5 dB or 10 dB (with BERs of 0.00001 or 0.0001), distortion may cause changes in the object size, but the overall shape is still preserved, and the number of points increases to varying degrees. However, when the SNR is higher, near-perfect PC transmission can be achieved by the traditional method. In contrast, for GenSeC-PC, even at 0 dB, rates of 128, 512, and 896 can achieve high semantic accuracy, and at a rate of 32, the basic shape of the object can still be preserved. Thus, in terms of signal-to-noise ratio, GenSeC-PC demonstrates its strong transmission robustness. Furthermore, in terms of bandwidth usage, GenSeC-PC exhibits superior transmission efficiency. Compared to traditional methods, GenSeC-PC can reduce the required bandwidth (in terms of transmitted symbols) to as little as 1/1000, or even 1/10000, of the original amount.


