% Baseline
In the baseline configuration \ref{ite:var_1}, only \R{\SI{7.08(0.05)}{\percent}} of the injections lead to functional errors, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:40_redmule_vulnerability} (Column 1).
This relatively low vulnerability can be attributed to the fact that not all parts of the circuit are actively used at any given time. 
If a transient fault occurs in an idle section, it does not necessarily propagate to the output, thereby mitigating the overall error rate.
% Data Protection
Introducing data protection in \ref{ite:var_2} significantly improves fault tolerance. 
Functional errors are significantly reduced, resulting in \R{\SI{99.36(0.02)}{\percent}} correct terminations.
Compared to the baseline \ref{ite:var_1}, most faults that previously resulted in functional errors now result in correct termination after recomputation.
% A key observation is that naturally masked faults do not trigger the retry circuitry. 
% This ensures that the total number of retries remains relatively low compared to the total number of injected faults. 
% Consequently, in environments with a high fault probability, the accelerator maintains similar performance levels compared to fault-free executions, as retries occur only when faults actively disrupt computations.
Consequently, the data protection of \RedMulE{} reduces the vulnerability by \resultvulnerability{} compared to the baseline implementation. 
% Full Protection
With the fully protected version of \RedMulE{} \ref{ite:var_3}, more than \R{\SI{99.9997}{\percent}} of injected faults result in correct termination with no functional errors after \R{\si{1}M} injections.
Approximately \R{\SI{12}{\percent}} of injected faults require a recomputation, indicating that the retry mechanism effectively handles detected errors by restarting operations when necessary.
Additionally, no timeouts were observed, suggesting that the fault recovery mechanism successfully mitigates control path corruption.

