\subsubsection{Axial stiffness reconstruction from statics}\label{s:Ex_Ten_A}
The planar sheet with length $L_x = 2L$ and width $B = L$ shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:Ex1_statDef}a~\&~b is considered. The sheet is loaded with a point load $P = fB = 500F$ at the right end ($X=4L$), and fixed at the left end ($X=0$). The chosen reference distribution of the axial stiffness is
\eqb{l}
\EA(\xi)/\EAR = \ds 2+0.5\cos(3\pi\xi) - \xi \,,
\label{e:Ex1A}\eqe 
where $\xi = X/2L$ and $\EAR = 100F$, see Fig.~\ref{fig:Ex1_statDef}c. In the inverse analysis, between 5 and 30 linear ME are used to approximate this material distribution, leading to $n_\mrvr = \mathinner{\text{6--31}}$ design variables. For the FE problem, the optimal convergence ratio, $O(h^3)$, is obtained as Fig.~\ref{fig:Ex1_statDef}d shows. Synthetic data is generated from 1020 FE, while all inverse analyses are conducted with 30 FE since its $L^2$ error is only $e_u\approx 10^{-5}$. 
