\begin{abstract}
Antenna modeling is a time-consuming and complex process, decreasing the speed of antenna analysis and design. In this paper, a \underline{l}arge language model (LLM)- \underline{e}nabled \underline{a}ntenna \underline{m}odeling method, called LEAM, is presented to address this challenge. LEAM enables automatic antenna model generation based on language descriptions via prompt input, images, descriptions from academic papers, patents, and technical reports (either one or multiple). The effectiveness of LEAM is demonstrated by three examples: a Vivaldi antenna generated from a complete user description, a slotted patch antenna generated from an incomplete user description and the operating frequency, and a monopole slotted antenna generated from images and descriptions scanned from the literature. For all the examples, correct antenna models are generated in a few minutes. The code can be accessed via https://github.com/TaoWu974/LEAM.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Large language model, antenna, electronic design automation, GPT, antenna modeling, human–computer interaction
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

In antenna research and applications, there is a demanding need for exploring novel antenna structures and reproducing designs from the literature. Antenna modeling is often unavoidable in this process, costing a lot of time and effort. Depending on the user’s experience, modeling a complex antenna could take from several tens of minutes to hours, and the number of such antenna modeling tasks grows rapidly with the growth of the antenna industry. It would be ideal to reduce or remove this repetitive and error-prone work. This inspires us to automate antenna modeling, speeding up the antenna design and innovation process.

It is routine to use the graphical user interface (GUI) in electromagnetic (EM) modeling and simulation tools for antenna modeling and visualization. These visualized antenna structures can be represented in macro files, which are command lines describing the model. For example, CST Microwave Studio employs Visual Basic for Applications (VBA) macros, and HFSS supports IronPython macros. Hence, by generating appropriate macro files, the antenna modeling process can become automated.

However, macro files are complex, and manual scripting is even more time-consuming than drawing the model using GUI. For example, importing material to an object is a straightforward click using the GUI of EM simulation tools, but tens of parameters are needed in the macro file. The gap between the simple language description of the user and the complex macro file that can correctly define the antenna model becomes the bottleneck, which has not been solved till now. However, a recent innovation, Large Language Models (LLMs), provides a promising solution, bridging simple descriptions in prompt (a prompt is the text input to the LLM) and complex macro files by its code generation \cite{lin2024llm}, geometry understanding \cite{parra2024can}, parameterization \cite{wu2023cad} and learning ability.

Although have not been used in antenna modeling, LLMs are investigated for automated macro scripting for 3D mechanical structure modeling. Some pioneer research works and findings are as follows. \cite{zamfirescu2023johnny} shows weak prompts can lead to poor responses and task failures. \cite{li2025llm4cad} shows that LLMs with vision capabilities (i.e., image recognition) outperform text-only LLMs as the modeling complexity increases. \cite{badagabettu2024query2cad} collects macro templates and benchmark test cases to assess LLM-based modeling techniques. SolidWorks employs LLMs in modeling mechanical components \cite{deng2024investigation}.

Despite the advancements in applying LLMs to 3D modeling, two limitations are restricting their applications in antenna modeling: (1) There is limited research considering multi-modal input (i.e., not only textual descriptions, but also image input) \cite{li2025llm4cad}, but is highly needed in LLM-enabled antenna modeling, especially for reproducing models from the literature. (2) Antenna modeling often consists of a series of solids' modeling and boolean operations among them, which is quite different from mechanical modeling's process of selecting one model and configuring its parameters \cite{li2025llm4cad}.

To address these challenges, the \underline{L}LM-\underline{e}nabled \underline{a}ntenna \underline{m}odeling method (LEAM) is proposed. The macro scripting process is divided into systematic steps, and a set of LLM tools based on OpenAI LLM Application Programming Interface (APIs) with designated prompts including responses from other LLM tools are designed for these steps. The input of LEAM is textual descriptions and images (if available), and the output is the antenna model. Currently, CST Microwave Studio is used as the EM simulator. To the best of our knowledge, this research is the first work investigating LLM-enabled antenna automated modeling. Moreover, LEAM is a prompt-only method, avoiding complex and time-consuming fine-tuning (i.e., additional training using specific data for antennas) or retrieval-augmented generation (i.e., providing knowledge databases, such as documents, and books), improving usability.


\section{The LEAM Methodology}
\subsection{The workflow of LEAM}
In LEAM, the antenna modeling approach is divided into systematic steps, and for each step, a tool is created. The tools are shown in Fig. \ref{fig:workflow} and Table \ref{tab:tools}. The user employs the tools mostly sequentially, except in the first step, there is selection, which will be detailed later.

