\section{Introduction}
The rapid growth of intelligent wireless services such as the Internet of Things (IoT), Mobile Edge Computing (MEC), and smart city applications is driving the evolution of future wireless networks toward large-scale Heterogeneous Networks (HetNets)~\cite{tian2024large}. These HetNets involve a large number of mobile users and dynamic environments with heterogeneous communication and computing resources. As a result, HetNets must handle massive data traffic, ensure low-latency responses, and maintain energy efficiency under diverse service demands, which introduces fundamental challenges for resource management and decision-making~\cite{agarwal2022comprehensive}. In particular, the coexistence of heterogeneous user types, diverse Quality-of-Service (QoS) requirements, and time-varying wireless conditions leads to high-dimensional state spaces, complex user-resource interactions, and non-stationary dynamics. These properties hinder the scalability and generalization of traditional optimization and supervised learning-based methods~\cite{zhou2024large}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width = 0.49\textwidth]{motivation_revised2.pdf}
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\caption{Comparison of DRL critic designs for HetNets. Conventional MLP-based critics (Part A) struggle with generalization in high-dimensional spaces. LLM-aided critics (Part B) improve expressiveness but face domain mismatch and integration challenges. Our proposed ReaCritic (Part C) introduces transformer-based bidirectional reasoning to achieve more accurate value estimation and scalable DRL training.}
\label{fig_mo}
\end{figure}
Faced with these challenges, Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) has gained attention as a data-driven approach for autonomous network management in complex wireless environments. By interacting with the environment and optimizing long-term rewards, DRL enables agents to adapt to dynamic network conditions without relying on predefined models~\cite{alwarafy2022frontiers}.
Its success in domains such as intelligent control, robotics, and wireless resource management demonstrates its potential for handling large-scale, uncertain systems~\cite{du2024enhancing,won2024resource,yang2024beyond}. 
However, when applied to large-scale HetNets, DRL still faces key limitations not only due to environmental complexity but also due to internal architectural bottlenecks. 
Although DRL algorithms are capable of learning adaptive behaviors through trial-and-error interaction, many existing designs rely on simple Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP)-based model structures that treat input states as flat feature vectors and apply direct approximations for policy or value functions. 
This formulation fundamentally lacks the capacity to capture latent dependencies among state variables or to represent tradeoffs inherent in multi-objective decision-making. 
In heterogeneous wireless environments, where metrics such as latency, throughput, and energy efficiency are often tightly coupled and evolve asynchronously, such limitations can lead to unstable training, slow convergence, and poor performance in dynamic or unseen scenarios. Taking actor-critic-based DRL algorithms as an example, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig_mo}, the critic network estimates Q-values based on environmental states and the actions selected by the actor network. However, due to the high dimensionality and structural complexity of both the HetNet states and the action space, this mapping becomes difficult and inaccurate, limiting the effectiveness of actor updates and ultimately degrading overall DRL performance.


% However, when applied to large-scale HetNets, conventional DRL algorithms encounter fundamental limitations due to the intrinsic properties of such environments. 
% In HetNets, the co-existence of diverse user types, rapidly fluctuating wireless channels, and heterogeneous resource demands leads to high-dimensional and non-stationary state spaces. These characteristics severely hinder the agent’s ability to perform efficient assignments and to extract generalizable patterns from experience.
% Furthermore, the presence of long-term temporal dependencies and asynchronous user behaviors makes it difficult to construct reliable value functions or policy mappings that remain effective across varying network states and user dynamics. As a result, the DRL training process is likely to converge slowly and get sub-optimal performance. Moreover, it is difficult for conventional DRL models to accurately capture the complex relationship between the system state and optimal resource control decisions, especially under non-stationary and uncertain conditions. In addition, the quality of policy evaluation and value estimation in DRL frameworks is often unreliable in such dynamic settings, which further exacerbates learning instability and degrades decision accuracy. These limitations call for the development of a more advanced DRL framework that can effectively reason over complex state representations, model relational dependencies among users and resources, and adaptively handle temporal variations in heterogeneous wireless networks.

