\subsubsection{Data description and metrics}
%
We use two datasets from Twitter and a one from a music platform, containing user activity logs while interacting with different content types. Note that social networks are characterized by user and cascade activity that are heterogeneous and non-stationary, \ie large differences in overall volume of events per user and per cascade, as well as temporal variations and bursts of the intensity of events. For the real datasets, the hyperparameters $\beta$ and $\tau$ are cross-validated (see \Tab{tab:cross-vals}).%

\inlinetitle{\texttt{music2} dataset \cite{zarezadeCorrelatedCascadesCompete2017}}{.}~It is a registry of Twitter users sharing URLs to Google Play and Youtube (taken as two cascades) of songs during one month of $2015$. The dataset is filtered so to keep the $\Nu = 93$ users with the highest adoption of these $\Nc=2$ cascades (more than $50$ tweets) which amounts to a total of $N_e = 79065$ events.
It is noted that the Youtube and Google Play related events of this dataset are neither uncorrelated nor in pure competition. As a matter of fact, beyond the market competition between streaming services, album releases cause a burst of interest on every platform.

\inlinetitle{\texttt{url} dataset \cite{hodasSimpleRulesSocial2014}}{.}~It contains tweets with URLs transformed by shortening services, commonly used when users share links on Twitter. We selected a (rather) stationary segment of the stream, and then we filtered the event log so as to keep $\Nu=637$ users related to the $\Nc=6$ cascades: \texttt{bit.ly}, \texttt{migre.me}, \texttt{tinyurl.com}, \texttt{tiny.ly}, \texttt{j.mp}, and \texttt{is.gd}, during $10$ days of $2010$, which amounts to a total of $N_e = 65321$ events.
%

\inlinetitle{\texttt{lastFM} dataset \cite{herradaMusicRecommendationDiscovery}}{.}~This is a registry of user listenings to music from the LastFM platform and spans over $T=6$ months. For the sake of visualization, we filter this dataset so as to keep the $N_c = 50$ most popular artists (taken as cascades), as well as $N_u = 559$ users with at least $50$ events each in those cascades. %
The final dataset is then composed of a total of $N_e = 56296$ listening events.


