\subsubsection{Results}

For the \texttt{music2} dataset (\Fig{sub:lglk_music2}), we first notice that both linear models, \IC and \MIClin, have the same learning behavior over different fractions of the test set, though \MIClin has a better test-likelihood score. Similarly for \CC and \MIC, which are equipped with Boltzmann mixing function, where %
\MIC performs better. %
Next, using the models learned on the training data, we generate event logs and compare them with the real test event logs. In \Fig{sub:inv_l1_music2}, we observe that the inverse $l_1$-distance is slightly better for linear models, however the Pearson correlation in \Fig{sub:corr_2_music2} suggests that \MIC replicates best the real event generation process. %


Regarding the \texttt{url} dataset, the test log-likelihoods plotted in \Fig{sub:lglk_url} indicate that most of the models present the same learning tendency, with \CC being slightly inferior. \Fig{sub:lglk_url} shows that all models benefit from as the training set grows (note that \IC is plotted exactly below \MIClin);
note that the number of events per user are here smaller than in \Fig{sub:lglk_music2}, also this dataset has greater complexity.
For the generation of events, \Fig{sub:inv_l1_url} indicates that even if the scores vary notably across the cascades, \MIC achieves a better approximation for $3$ out of the $6$ cascades, and is overall a little below linear models. Nevertheless, the Pearson correlation in \Fig{sub:corr_url} shows that, cascade-wise, \MIC is better or comparable to the other models, and overall exhibits a higher correlation to the real data. It is worth noting that \IC and \CC obtain negative scores for the $2$nd and the $6$th cascade. To qualitatively assess the performance of the model, we also compare the test intensity to the one sampled from the learned model, as well as the theoretical expected intensity. \Fig{fig:ints_url} shows that the empirical conditional intensities over time of \MIC model are in agreement with the real test data, indicating an agreement between our numerical and analytical results in the context of the \texttt{url} dataset. %

The results for the \texttt{lastfm} dataset are displayed in \Fig{fig:eval_lastfm}. The test log-likelihood in %
Nonlinear models appear to have a superior test log-likelihood score than linear ones, with \MIC being the model with the best performance. Given the large number of cascades, we omit plotting for this dataset the inverse $l_1$-distance and Pearson correlation measures. Nevertheless, the larger diversity of this dataset can provide insights regarding the distribution of cascade popularity shown in \Fig{sub:rank_c_lastfm} in log-scale. There, \MIC exhibits a more accurate distribution of events per cascade compared to the other models, both in terms of number of events generated and in its ordered shape.
%
This figure suggests that none of the models gets sufficiently close to the test data, especially for the most popular cascades, hence they seem unable to capture the full complexity of this dataset. In fact, for the \texttt{lastfm} dataset, the largest event counts mostly originate from exogenous factors, such as the release of a new album or events like concerts.
