\subsection{Bi-layered visualization of social network activity}\label{sec:vizualization}

This section meets the promise made by the schematic view in \Fig{fig:adoption_scheme}, by demonstrating that \MIC model can indeed produce such insightful visualizations for real data. Before discussing the results displayed in \Fig{fig:all_vis}, we should note that representing social network activity data as a bi-layered network, one layer for the users and another for the cascades, is intuitive, natural, and has been used in prior exploratory studies \cite{dedomenicoMuxVizToolMultilayer2015,loganSocialNetworkAnalysis2023}. The contribution of our work in this aspect stems from the fact that \MIC parameters $\mathbf{\Theta}=(\mathbf{\Sigma}, \mathbf{M}, \mathbf{\Weight})$
can describe this structure, therefore such a bi-layered visualization can be computed numerically within our modeling framework by inferring the model parameters for real data.

In the first row of \Fig{fig:all_vis}, the \texttt{url} and \texttt{lastfm} datasets are visualized using a typical bi-layer spring layout based of the real data. The adjacency matrix ($\mathbf{W} = \mathbf{A}$) gives the user graph at top layer, and the cascade activity is at the bottom layer, though lacking structure as this is not provided by the data ($\Sigma = \mathbf{\Id}$).
Node size is proportional to the associated volume of events, by users or cascades, and the node color of a user illustrates the mixture of his activity regarding the cascades.

In the second row, in \Fig{sub:visu_a_url} and \ref{sub:visu_a_lastfm}, we provide the bi-layered visualizations computed based on the \MIC model, specifically using the respective inferred parameters $\mathbf{\tilde{\Theta}}=(\mathbf{\tilde{\Sigma}}, \mathbf{\tilde{M}}, \mathbf{\tilde{\Weight}})$. %
Edges within the cascade (resp. user) layer exist only if they are inferred in $\mathbf{\tilde{\Sigma}}$ (resp. $\mathbf{\tilde{\Weight}}$), also are drawn as being undirected for simplicity. Dashed edges between the two layers correspond to the highest value of the cascade-to-user coupling intensity $\mathbf{\tilde{M}}_{(\cdot)}^{(c)}$ for each cascade $c$. The position of cascades is computed according to their inferred interactions $\mathbf{\tilde{\Sigma}}$. The position of each user $u$ is computed based on his activity with regards to the cascades. Specifically, the learned cascade mixing function $f_u(\cdot|t)$ maps user $u$'s activity to a probability distribution over the cascades, which is then transformed into coordinates in the bottom layer.
%
\Fig{sub:visu_a_url} visualizes the \texttt{url} dataset where specific cascades (\texttt{bit.ly} and \texttt{tinyurl}) appear to be central, as also noted in previous works for this dataset \cite{valeraModelingAdoptionUsage2015}. %
Links between shortening services are uncovered: for instance, the weighted cascades interactions, depicted with arrows at the bottom layer, can illustrate the varying popularity of URL shortening services shared on Twitter. %
Additionally, this graphical view %
highlights the interplay between users and cascades, as it can shed light on the coupling between network structure and topic alignment, \eg to reveal clusters of users and cascades that are closely related in terms of content \cite{aielloFriendshipPredictionHomophily2012}%
. In fact, for the \texttt{url} dataset we indeed observe communities of users to be well-organized around specific URL shortening services, and migrating users to be spatially located in transition zones between cascades.

\Fig{sub:visu_a_lastfm} visualizes a different landscape for the \texttt{lastfm} dataset. %
The higher number of cascades leads to a more dense network structure, with highly central artists (\eg $c_1$ and $c_3$). Nevertheless, the inferred network presents a typical %
center-periphery-isolated structure, %
as well as hubs connecting with distinct communities (\eg $c_1$). The %
stronger mixing of cascades through the user social network is most possibly due to %
the diversity of music tastes and %
cross-genre listening among users.
\vfil

