\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we have presented the \textit{\methodName} (\MIC) model, aiming at providing a more accurate modeling tool for the underlying information diffusion mechanisms in OSNs. The use of a marked point process framework ensures an easily interpretable and more refined description of the coupling between information cascades and user activity. Benefiting from the convex and factorization properties of the likelihood, parameter learning can be performed in parallel for each user via Maximum Likelihood Estimation methods. The model is also supported by a theoretical framework that allows the derivation of differential equations of the moments of the number of events and the conditional intensity, which provide analytical insights for the understanding of the underlying information diffusion dynamics.


The presented experiments provided a comprehensive evaluation of the \MIC model, using synthetic and real data, several evaluation measures, and from various viewpoints. The design of an ablation study, as well as the comparison with its competitors quantitatively supports the different contributions of \MIC. The learned model is also compared to the event generating process from a numerical and analytical perspective. The results suggests that \MIC manages to bridge the gap between theoretical and numerical approaches to the problem of modeling intricate patterns of information diffusion. We finally demonstrated that \MIC can be used to visualize insightfully social network activity in a bi-layered network. %
Such representation highlights %
the interplay between users and cascades, with a focus on cascade interactions that shape the underlying structure of information pathways. %
Further investigation can be concentrated on improving the accuracy of the model by refining some modeling choices and the parameter estimation method. For instance, one could consider introducing time-dependent parameters ($\Theta_t$) and non-parametric triggering kernels \cite{zhouLearningTriggeringKernels2013,lemonnierMultivariateHawkesProcesses2017}. Data preprocessing can be beneficial for treating non-stationarity and/or heterogeneity, \eg using existing methods to detect change-points in Hawkes processes \cite{zhangConjugateBayesianTwostep2024}, or deal with parameter learning given unbalanced data availability \cite{salehiLearningHawkesProcesses2019a}. %
Additional work could %
elaborate further the theoretical properties of the model.


