\subsection{A path with an even number of points: Details}
\label{even-path-details}

Recall the set $P$ of $2k$ points, $k\ge 3$, on the $x$-axis, described in the previous section and illustrated in Figure~\ref{path-even-fig}(a). We say that an edge $e$ {\em intersects the $y$-axis} if the intersection of $e$ and the $y$-axis is not empty (the intersection could be an endpoint of $e$).
{\color{mycolor} 
The longest paths for points on a line were characterized in Lemma~\ref{path-lemma}.
}Denote by $\cal H$ the set of all longest spanning paths on $P$.

\begin{lemma}
	\label{epsilon-lemma}
	Let $0\le \varepsilon \le \frac{1}{8k}$ be a real number. Suppose that every point in $P$ is perturbed by a distance of at most $\varepsilon$. Let $P'$ be the new point set after perturbation. Then, the order of the points along any longest path for $P'$ is the same as the order of the points along some path in $\cal H$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	The length of any path on $P$ is an integer. Therefore, any path in $\cal H$ is at least 1 unit longer than any path not in $\cal H$. 


	Let  $H'$ be any longest path on $P'$. 
	The difference between its length and the length of any path in $\cal H$ is at most $(2k{-}1)\cdot2\varepsilon$ because $H'$ has $2k{-}1$ edges, each edge has $2$ endpoints, and each endpoint is at distance at most $\varepsilon$ from its corresponding point in $P$.
	Since $\varepsilon \le \frac{1}{8k}$ the difference is less than $1/2$. Therefore, $H'$ cannot correspond to a path that is not in $\cal H$, so $H'$ corresponds to a path in $\cal H$  with the same order of points.  
\end{proof}

Our plan is to assign new $y$-coordinates to the points of $P$ to obtain a point set $P'$ for which the longest path is $y$-monotone and unique. The new $y$-coordinates will be at most $\frac{1}{8k}$, and thus, by Lemma~\ref{epsilon-lemma}, the longest path $H'$ of $P'$ will correspond to a path in $\cal H$. We will make $H'$ correspond to the path $p_1,p_{-2}, p_2,p_{-3},p_3,\allowbreak\dots,p_k,p_{-1}$,  which is in $\cal{H}$ (by Lemma~\ref{path-lemma}) and depicted in Figure~\ref{path-even-fig}(b).  We assign to each point $p_i$ the $y$-coordinate $y_i$ such that the following holds: % and denote the resulting point set by $P'$: 
\[y_1\gg y_{-2}\gg y_2\gg y_{-3}\gg y_3\gg \cdots\gg  y_{-k}\gg y_k\gg y_{-1}.\]
We set $y_1=\frac{1}{8k}$, $y_{-1}=0$, and use the following lemma to identify the remaining $y$-coordinates.

\begin{lemma}
	\label{delta-lemma}
	There exists a real number $\delta$, with $0<\delta < y_1$, such that if $0\le y_i \le\delta$ for each $i\neq 1$ then the longest path on $P'$ connects $p_1$ to $p_{-2}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since each $y_i$ is at most $1/8k$, Lemma~\ref{epsilon-lemma} implies that any longest path $H'$ on $P'$ corresponds to a path $H$ in $\cal H$. Due to small $y$-coordinates, we have $|H'|=|H|+\Delta(H')$ for some small value $\Delta(H')\geq 0$ which depends on the new $y$-coordinates. {\color{mycolor} Specifically, we have 
\begin{align*}
|H'|& =\sum_{(p_i,p_j)\in E(H')} |p_ip_j| 
    =\sum_{(p_i,p_j)\in E(H')} \sqrt{|i-j|^2+|y_i-y_j|^2}\\ 
    &=|H|+\sum_{(p_i,p_j)\in E(H')}\left( \sqrt{|i-j|^2+|y_i-y_j|^2} -|i-j|\right)
     =|H|+\Delta(H'),
\end{align*}
where $0\leq \sqrt{|i-j|^2+|y_i-y_j|^2}-|i-j|\leq |y_i-y_j| \leq \max\{y_i,y_j\}$.}

Recall from Lemma~\ref{path-lemma} that $p_1$ is an endpoint of any longest path in $\cal H$. Moreover, $p_1$ is connected to a point (different from $p_{-1}$) to the left of the $y$-axis. For $j\in\{2,\dots,k\}$ let $\ell_{j}$ be the Euclidean distance between $p_1$ and the point $(-j,0)$, and let $\Delta_{j}$ be the difference of their $x$-coordinates as in Figure~\ref{path-even-fig}(a). 
 The contribution of $p_1p_{-j}$ to $|H'|$ would be at least $\ell_j-\delta$ (when $p_{-j}$ has $y$-coordinate $\delta$) and at most $\ell_j$ (when $p_{-j}$ has $y$-coordinate $0$). The contribution of the corresponding edge to $|H|$ would be $\Delta_j$.
Hence the contribution of $p_1p_{-j}$ to $\Delta(H')$ would be at least $\ell_j-\delta-\Delta_j$ and at most $\ell_j-\Delta_j$. 
{\color{mycolor} An easy calculation shows} %Observe 
that 
\[\ell_{2}-
	\Delta_{2}>\ell_{3}-
	\Delta_{3}>\dots > \ell_{k}-
	\Delta_{k};\]
this is also implied by the fact that $\Delta_{i+1}{-}\Delta_i=1$ while $\ell_{i+1}{-}\ell_{i}<1$. If we set $\delta < (\ell_{2}-
	\Delta_{2})-(\ell_{3}-
	\Delta_{3})$, then the contribution of $p_1p_{-2}$ to $\Delta(H')$ is at least \[\ell_{2}-\delta-
	\Delta_{2}>\ell_{2}-
	\Delta_{2}-((\ell_{2}-
	\Delta_{2})-(\ell_{3}-
	\Delta_{3}))=\ell_{3}-
	\Delta_{3},\] which is larger than the contribution of any other {\color{mycolor}plausible} edge $p_1p_{-j}$.  {\color{mycolor}Since the $y$-coordinates of all other points are less than $\delta$, any other edge of $H'$ contributes less than $\delta$ to $\Delta(H')$.}
	By setting \[\delta=\frac{(\ell_2-\Delta_2)-(\ell_3-\Delta_3)}{2k-1},\] the contribution of $p_1p_{-2}$ 
  {\color{mycolor}exceeds} %would be even larger than 
 the sum of the contributions of the remaining $2k-2$ edges of $H'$. 
 Thus, for this choice of $\delta$ the longest path $H'$ connects $p_1$ to $p_{-2}$.
\end{proof}

By Lemma~\ref{delta-lemma}, we have a specific value $\delta$ such that the longest path includes edge  $(p_1,p_{-2})$. Now we set $y_{-2}=\delta$ and repeat the arguments of Lemma~\ref{delta-lemma}, with $y_{-2}$ and $p_{-2}$ (instead of $y_1$ and $p_1$). 
