we obtain the unique longest path $p_1,p_{-2}, p_2,p_{-3},p_3,\dots,p_k,p_{-1}$, as in Figure~\ref{path-even-fig}(b); in each of the last two steps, there is only one remaining plausible edge (namely, $p_{-k} p_k$ from $p_{-k}$, and $p_k p_{-1}$ from $p_k$).} This path is $y$-monotone and hence is noncrossing.

\subsection{A path with an odd number of points}
\label{odd-path}

In this section, we obtain a noncrossing longest path with an odd number of points. Here is the place where we use the  coordinate $(0,0)$ of the point $p_1\in P$. {\color{mycolor} We show that our construction for even paths leads to a construction for odd paths by simply removing $p_1$. Thus we do not need to repeat the lemmas of Section~\ref{even-path-details} for the odd case.}
