First we observe that our path construction can be generalized to any set of $x$-coordinates.  

\begin{lemma}\label{evenpath-lem}
 For every even integer $n\ge 4$, every set $P$ of $n$ real numbers, and every $\delta>0$ such that the $\delta$-neighborhood of the median of $P$ does not contain any points in $P$, 
 there exists a set $P'$ of $n$ points in the plane with the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item the $x$-projection of $P'$ is $P$;
   \item all $y$-coordinates are in the interval $[0,\delta]$;
    \item the $x$-projection of any longest path on $P'$ is a longest path on $P$;
    \item the longest spanning path on $P'$ is unique and  noncrossing; and
    \item the $y$-coordinates of the two endpoints of the longest path are $0$ and $\delta$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
We choose the points in $P'$ such that their $x$-coordinates are the same as the numbers in $P$ and their $y$-coordinates are in $[0,\delta]$, and thus (1) and (2) follow.

By an argument similar to the proof of Lemma~\ref{path-lemma}(i) one can show that the difference of lengths of a longest and a non-longest path on $P$ is at least $2\delta$. Therefore Lemma~\ref{epsilon-lemma} would imply that by choosing the $y$-coordinates in the interval $[0,2\delta/8k]$, any longest path on $P'$ corresponds to a longest path on $P$, and thus (3) follows. Items (4) and (5) follow by proper choices of $y$-coordinates similar to that of Lemma~\ref{delta-lemma}.
\end{proof}

