From Lemma~\ref{cycle-lemma} all edges of any longest cycle on $P$ intersect $p_1$ and the two edges incident to $p_1$ lie on opposite sides of it. In particular $p_\infty$ must be  connected to two points in the set $\{p_1,p_{-1},p_{-2},\dots, p_{-k}\}$. 

Any longest cycle on $P$ has an integer length and is at least 1 unit longer than any cycle on $P$ that is not longest. 
We will change the $y$-coordinates of points in $P$ by at most $1/16k$ and obtain a new point set $P'$ for which the longest cycle is unique and noncrossing. 
By arguments similar to Lemma~\ref{epsilon-lemma} and Lemma~\ref{cycle-epsilon-lemma} a longest cycle $C'$ on $P'$ corresponds to a longest cycle $C$ on $P$. Moreover, we have $|C'|=|C|+\Delta(C')$ for some small value $\Delta(C')\geq 0$ which depends on the new $y$-coordinates. We set $y_\infty=-1/16k$. This is the only negative $y$-coordinate. We choose a parameter $\delta\in(0,1/16k)$ such that if we change the $y$-coordinates of all other points by at most $\delta$  then the longest cycle would connect $p_\infty$ to $p_1$ and $p_{-1}$. We then set $y_1=\delta$ and $y_{-1}=0$. The other $y$-coordinates will be assigned by repeated applications of Lemma~\ref{epsilon-lemma} to get the longest path of Section~\ref{even-path-details} with endpoints $p_1$ and $p_{-1}$. The longest cycle $C'$ would then be the concatenation of this path with the segments $p_\infty p_1$ and $p_\infty p_{-1}$.


We choose $\delta$ as follows. For $j\in\{1,\dots,k\}$ let $\ell_{j}$ be the Euclidean distance between $p_\infty$ and the point $(-j,0)$, and let $\Delta_{j}$ be the difference of their $x$-coordinates. The contribution of $p_\infty p_{-j}$ to $\Delta(C')$ would be at least $\ell_j-\Delta_j$ and at most $\ell_j-\Delta_j+\delta$.  Let $\ell_{0}$ be the Euclidean distance between $p_\infty$ and the point $(0,0)$, and let $\Delta_{0}$ be the difference of their $x$-coordinates. The contribution of $p_\infty p_{1}$ to $\Delta(C')$ is at least $\ell_0-\Delta_0$. Then
\[\ell_{0}-
	\Delta_{0}>\ell_{1}-
	\Delta_{1}>\ell_{2}-
	\Delta_{2}>\dots > \ell_{k}-
	\Delta_{k}.\]
By setting $\delta < (\ell_{1}-
	\Delta_{1})-(\ell_{2}-
	\Delta_{2})$ the contribution of $p_\infty p_{-1}$ to $\Delta(C')$ is at least $\ell_{1}-	\Delta_{1}$, which is strictly greater than $\ell_{2}-	\Delta_{2}+\delta$ and thus also greater than the contribution of any $p_\infty p_{-j}$ for $j\in\{2,3,\dots,k\}$.  The contribution of each such $p_\infty p_{-j}$ is at most $\delta$ because the $y$-coordinate of $p_{-j}$ is at most $\delta$.
	Thus, by setting \[\delta=\frac{(\ell_1-\Delta_1)-(\ell_2-\Delta_2)}{2k+1},\] the contribution of each of $p_\infty p_{1}$ and $p_\infty p_{-1}$ exceeds
 the sum of the contributions of the remaining $2k{-}1$ edges of $C'$. 
 Thus, for this choice of $\delta$ the  cycle $C'$ connects $p_\infty$ to $p_{1}$ and $p_{-1}$. Other edges of $C'$ form a longest path with endpoints $p_1$ and $p_{-1}$ as obtained in Section~\ref{even-path-details}. To ensure that $p_\infty p_1$ does not cross other edges we choose $x_\infty$ sufficiently large so that the points $p_2,p_3,\dots,p_k$ lie below the segment $p_\infty p_1$. This is possible because the $y$-coordinate of all these points is smaller than $y_1$.
