Here, we exhibit an alternative point set for which the longest perfect matching is noncrossing.
Our construction follows the same framework as for paths and cycles. 
Let $P$ be a set of $2k$ points $p_i=(i,0)$ for $ i=\pm1,\pm2,\dots,\pm k$. One can verify that a perfect matching on $P$ is longest if and only if all edges cross the $y$-axis. One such matching is $M=\{p_{-i}p_i\colon i=1,\dots,k\}$. Using ideas similar to those used for paths and cycles, one can assign to each $p_i$ a new $y$-coordinate $y_i$ to make $M$ longest and noncrossing at the same time; see Figure~\ref{matching-fig}. The new $y$-coordinates are of the following form: $y_1\gg y_{-1}=y_2\gg y_{-2}=y_3\gg\cdots= y_{k}\gg y_{-k}$. 


