\section{Alternative approach: Failed}
\label{cycle-section-failed}


\subsection{A cycle with even number of points: An overview}
Let $P$ be a set of $n=2k$ points, with $k\ge 3$, on the $x$-axis such that $p_i=(i,0)$ for $i=\pm1,\pm2,\dots,\pm k$ as in Figure~\ref{cycle-even-fig-failed}. Our construction for cycles is somewhat similar to that of paths in the sense that our cycle consists of two $y$-monotone interior-disjoint paths between $p_1$ and $p_k$ (if $n$ is a multiple of $4$) or between $p_1$ and $p_{-k}$ (if $n$ is not a multiple of $4$). Although the main idea sounds simple, the noncrossing property of the longest cycle is not straightforward and it involves much more details.  


To simplify the indexing (of points and $y$-coordinates) from now on we assume that $n$ is a multiple of $4$, and hence the two paths will be between $p_1$ and $p_k$. Our construction for $n$ not being a multiple of $4$ is similar but with different indexing because in this case the  paths end at $p_{-k}$.

We observe that a spanning cycle on $P$ is longest if and only if all of its edges intersect the $y$-axis. Let $\cal C$ be the set of all longest spanning cycles on $P$.
We obtain a point set $P'$ by assigning to each point $p_i$ the $y$-coordinate $y_i$ such that (see Figure~\ref{cycle-even-fig-failed}): 
$$1/4k=y_1\gg y_{-2}\gg y_3\gg y_{-4}\gg y_5\gg \cdots\gg y_{k-1}\gg y_{-k}\gg y_k=0.$$
We choose $y_{-1}$ such that $p_{-1}$ lies just below (almost on) the segment $(p_1,p_{-2})$. For each $i\in\{2, 4, \dots, k-2\}$ we choose $y_i$ such that $p_i$ lies just below (almost on) the segment $(p_{-i+1},p_{i+1})$, and for each $i\in\{-3,-5, \dots, -(k-1)\}$ we choose $y_i$ such that  $p_i$ lies just below (almost on) the segment $(p_{-i-1},p_{i-1})$.

Due to the small $y$-coordinates, any longest cycle $C'$ on $P'$ corresponds to a cycle $C\in \cal C$. Moreover $|C'|=|C|+\Delta(C')$ for some small value $\Delta(C')$ which depends on the new $y$-coordinates.  Since $p_1$ has the largest $y$-coordinate, the contribution of the two edges of $C'$ that are incident to $p_1$ (say $e_1$ and $e_2$) is maximized when they are connected to the nearest plausible neighbors which are $p_{-1}$ and $p_{-2}$. We will choose the $y$-coordinates in such a way that the contribution of $e_1$ and $e_2$ is larger than the sum of the contributions of the remaining edges of the cycle. Thus $C'$ must have $e_1$ and $e_2$. Similarly, by suitable choice of $y$-coordinates, we enforce $C'$ to connect $p_{-1},p_{-2}$ to the nearest plausible neighbors which are $p_2,p_3$, and so on. By repeating this process, the longest cycle $C'$ would be the concatenation of two paths $p_1,p_{-2}, p_2, p_{-4},p_4,\dots,p_k$ and $p_1,p_{-1},p_3,p_{-3}, p_5,\dots,p_k$. 


