\subsection{A cycle with even number of points: Details}


Recall the point set $P$ from the previous section. Since $P$ has the same number of points on each side of the $y$-axis, the arguments in the proof of Lemma~\ref{path-lemma}-part (i) carry over to spanning cycles on $P$. Therefore, we can get the following lemma. 

\begin{lemma}
	\label{cycle-lemma-failed} A spanning cycle on $P$ is longest if and only if each of its edges intersects the $y$-axis.
\end{lemma}

Let $\cal C$ be the set of all longest spanning cycles on $P$.
The following lemma can be proved similar to Lemma~\ref{epsilon-lemma}, noting that the cycle has $2k$ edges instead. 

\begin{lemma}
	\label{cycle-epsilon-lemma-failed}
	Let $0\le \varepsilon \le 1/(4k+1)$ be a real number. Suppose that every point of $P$ moves to a point of distance at most $\varepsilon$. Then the order of the points along any longest cycle of the new point set is the same as the order of the points along some cycle in $\cal C$.
\end{lemma}


To obtain $P'$ we only need to identify the following $y$-coordinates with the given constraints:
$$y_1\gg y_{-2}\gg y_3\gg y_{-4}\gg y_5\gg \cdots\gg y_{k-1}\gg y_{-k}\gg y_k.$$ 
The $y$-coordinate for the remaining indices then would follow, as described in previous section. 
we set $y_1=1/(4k+1)$, $y_{k}=0$. We use the following lemma (which can be proved similar to Lemma~\ref{delta-lemma}) to assign the $y$-coordinates. 

\begin{lemma}
	\label{cycle-delta-lemma-failed}
	There exists a real number $0<\delta \ll y_1$ such that if $0\le y_i \le\delta$ for each $i\in\{-2,3,-4,5,\dots,k-1,-k,k\}$ then the longest cycle connects $p_1$ to $p_{-1}$ and $p_{-2}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{cycle-epsilon-lemma-failed} implies that the longest cycle $C'$ on $P'$ corresponds to a cycle $C$ in $\cal C$. Due to small $y$-coordinates, we have $|C'|=|C|+\Delta(C')$ for some small value $\Delta(C')$ which depends on the new $y$-coordinates. Lemma~\ref{cycle-lemma-failed} implies that $C'$ connects $p_1$ to two points to the left of the $y$-axis. Similar to Lemma~\ref{delta-lemma} define $\ell_{j}$ as the Euclidean distance between $p_1$ and the point $(0,-j)$, and define $\Delta_{j}$ as the difference of their $x$-coordinates for $j\in\{2,\dots,k\}$ (we will take care of index $-1$ later).



Similarly the contribution of $(p_1,p_{-j})$ to $|C'|$ would be at least $\ell_j-\delta$ and at most $\ell_j$. The contribution of the corresponding edge to $|C|$ is $\Delta_j$.
Hence the contribution of $(p_1,p_{-j})$ to $\Delta(C')$ would be at least $\ell_j-\delta-\Delta_j$ and at most $\ell_j-\Delta_j$. 


Notice that all due to the statement of the lemma and our placement of points, all points (except $p_{-1}$) have $y$-coordinate at most $\delta$.


Observe that 
	$$\ell_{2}-
	\Delta_{2}>\ell_{3}-
	\Delta_{3}>\dots > \ell_{k}-
	\Delta_{k}.$$



	Due to small $y$-coordinates the length of the longest cycle $C'$ on $P'$ is equal to the length of its corresponding cycle $C\in \cal C$ (which exists by Lemma~\ref{cycle-epsilon-lemma-failed}) plus a small value $\Delta(C')$.

	In $C'$, the point $p_1$ is connected to two points to the left of the $y$-axis (by Lemma~\ref{cycle-lemma-failed}). 	
	Similar to the proof of Lemma~\ref{delta-lemma} let $\ell_{j}$ and $\ell'_j$ be the Euclidean distances between $p_1$ and the points $p_j$ and $p'_j$ to the left of the $y$-axis, and let $\Delta_{j}$ and $\Delta'_j$ be the difference of their $x$-coordinates. The contribution $\ell_1 -\Delta_1$ of $(p_1,p_{-1})$ and $\ell'_1 -\Delta'_1$ of $(p_1,p'_{-1})$ to $\Delta(C')$ would be larger than the contribution of other edges. Thus by choosing 
	 $$\delta=\frac{(\ell_1+\ell'_1-\Delta_1-\Delta'_1)-(\ell_2+\ell'_2-\Delta_2-\Delta'_2)}{2k-2},$$ the contribution of $(p_1,p_{-1})$ and $(p_1,p'_{-1})$ would be larger than the sum of the contributions of the remaining $2k-2$ edges of $C'$. Thus, for this choice of $\delta$ the cycle $C'$ connects $p_1$ to $p_{-1}$ and $p'_{-1}$.
\end{proof}

