\section{Grassroots Consensus}\label{section:protocol}

\subsection{Blocklace Preliminaries}

We use $a\ne b \in X$ as a shorthand for $a\in X \wedge b\in X\wedge a\ne b$.
%
We assume a set of \emph{agents} $\Pi$, each endowed with a unique key-pair and identified by its public key $p\in \Pi$.
While $\Pi$ is potentially-infinite, we refer only to finite subsets $P\subset \Pi$ of it.

A \emph{$p$-block} $b$, with $p\in \Pi$, is a triple $b=(h,x,H)$ where $h$ is the hash of the pair $(x,H)$ signed by $p$, referred to as the \emph{identifier} of $b$, $x$ is an arbitrary \emph{payload}, which may be \emph{empty}, $x=\bot$, in which case we refer to $b$ as an \emph{empty block}, and $H$ a finite set of block identifiers, the blocks of which are \emph{pointed to} by $b$, with $b$ called \emph{initial} if $H=\emptyset$.  
An encoding of such a triple $b=(h,x,H)$ via a sequence of bits in an agreed-upon form is referred to as a \emph{well-formed block}.
We assume the hash function is collision-free whp and cryptographic, so that blocks cannot form cycles and we may identify a block $b$ with its identifier $h$. Furthermore, the method of signature allows the public key $p$ to be recovered from a signature by $p$.  


A block $b$ \emph{observes} itself, any block $b'$ pointed to  by $b$, and any block observed by $b'$.  The `observes' relation induces a partial order on any set of blocks.
Two blocks that do not observe each other are \emph{conflicting}, and  
if by the same agent they are \emph{equivocating}. %, else \emph{colliding}.  
%We say that a block $b\in B$ is \emph{non-conflicting} in $B$ if it does not conflict with any block $b'\in B$.
A block $b$ \emph{approves} a block $b'$ if $b$ observes $b'$ and does not observe any block equivocating with $b'$, and a set of blocks $B$ \emph{approves} $b'$ if every $b\in B$ approves $b'$.

The \emph{closure} of a block $b$, denoted $[b]$, is the set of blocks observed by $b$. 
If a block variable $b$ 
%\andy{previously, $\bot$ meant `empty', not `undefined'} 
is undefined then we deem its closure $[b]:=\emptyset$ to be empty rather than undefined.
For a set of blocks $B$ the \emph{closure} $[B]$ is defined by $[B] := \bigcup_{b\in B} [b]$, which is also the set of blocks \emph{observed by} $B$.
A set of blocks $B$ is \emph{closed} if $[b]\subseteq B$ for every $b\in B$, equivalently if $B=[B]$. 
A \emph{blocklace} is a closed set of blocks.  

To help avoid confusion, we use $B$ below to denote a closed set of blocks (a blocklace) and $D$ to denote any set of blocks, not necessarily closed.
Given a set of blocks $D$, a pointer to a block $b$ is \emph{dangling} in $D$ if $b\notin D$.  Thus, a blocklace is a set of blocks with no dangling pointers. A block $b\in D$ is a \emph{tip} of $D$ if no other block $b'\ne b\in D$ observes $b$.
%, and is a \emph{leaf} of $D$ if it observes no block in $D$.  
%\idit{Removed leaf as I believe it is not used.}

The \emph{depth} $d(b)$ of a block $b$ is $1$ if $b$ is initial else $d(b)=d(b')+1$ where $b'$ is a maximal-depth block pointed to by $b$.   The \emph{depth} $d(B)$ of a set of blocks $B$ is defined by $d(B) := \max_{b\in B} d(b)$.
A \emph{round} $r\ge 1$ in  $B$ is the set of all blocks $b\in B$ of depth $d(b)=r$, 
and the \emph{$r$-prefix of $B$}, $B_r$, consists of all blocks $b\in B$ with depth  $d(b)\le r$, equivalently all rounds in $B$ up to and including $r$.  Note that, by definition, all blocks of the same round do not observe each other. % and hence conflict with each other.
