\subsection{Protocol Concepts}
%\idit{I would rename this section as `Protocol Concepts' or something like that, because it's beyond preliminaries, it gets to the crux of the logic of the protocol.}

\mypara{Models}  We consider two models.
The first is the standard model of \emph{eventual synchrony} (aka \emph{partial synchrony)} that assumes a reliable network in which every message sent among correct agents is eventually received.  Message latency is unbounded, but in every run there is a time called the \emph{Global Stabilization Time (GST)}, which is unknown to the agents, after which all messages arrive within a least upper bound $\delta$ time for some 
%unknown 
$\delta >0$.  In particular, all messages sent any time before GST arrive by time GST+$\delta$. 

In the second model 
%is similar to eventual synchrony except that 
the assumption of a reliable network is relaxed. It assumes an \emph{eventually-reliable network}, in which any message sent infinitely often between correct agents eventually arrives, and a message sent after GST between correct agents arrives within $\delta$. This is  the model assumed in the original Paxos paper~\cite{lamport10.1145/279227.279229part-time}
and many sequels.
%; but no assumption is made on any individual message sent before GST.  Lacking a better name, 
We refer to this model as the \emph{UDP-ready} model.


%\andy{Need to comment that this is a model of partial synchrony that people sometimes consider, i.e., messages sent before GST can be lost forever. Can try to find references, but maybe Idit knows.}

\mypara{Parameters}  The protocol employs three key parameters $(P,\sigma,\Delta)$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $P\subset\Pi$,  a set of agents, 
    \item $\frac{1}{2}\le \sigma < 1$, a fraction, used to specify a supermajority among the agents 
    %\andy{seems like it's a number rather than a set of agents, but is later used to specify what we mean by a supermajority?}, 
    \item $\Delta>0$, a presumed upper-bound on message delay after GST, known to the agents.
\end{enumerate}
We refer to the parameters $(P,\sigma,\Delta)$ employed by the protocol for a given block $b$ or blocklace $B$, with values as above, as the \emph{prevailing constitution} in $b$, resp.\ $B$; when the value of $\Delta$ is immaterial we may refer to the prevailing constitution $(P,\sigma)$. We begin by assuming the prevailing constitution to consist of arbitrary constant values for all blocks during the protocol run, as is usually the case.  Later, in Section \ref{section:constitution}, we augment the protocol with constitutional amendment blocks by which the prevailing constitution may be amended.
%, and thus evolve during the run. % of the protocol.


\mypara{Supermajorities} Given agents $P\subset \Pi$, $|P|=n$, and $\frac{1}{2}\le \sigma < 1$, a \emph{$\sigma$-supermajority}  among $P$ is a fraction $Q\subseteq P$ such that $|Q| > \sigma n$, or, equivalently, that $\frac{|Q|}{|P|}>\sigma$.
A set of blocks $D$ is referred to as a \emph{$\sigma$-supermajority} if there is a $\sigma$-supermajority $Q\subseteq P$ such that for every $q\in Q$, $D$ includes at least one $q$-block.

Typically, a permissioned consensus protocol assumes that there are at most $f<n$ faulty agents among $P$. Given $f$, we say that a blocklace $B$ is $f$-\emph{safe} if it includes equivocations by at most $f$ agents, a block $b$ is $f$-\emph{safe} if $[b]$ is $f$-safe, and define $\sigma := \frac{n+f}{2n}$.
For example, if $f=0$ then  $\sigma = \frac{1}{2}$;
if $f=\frac{1}{2}n$ then $\sigma = \frac{3}{4}$; 
if $n = 3f+1$ (the standard assumption), then $\sigma = \frac{n+\frac{n-1}{3}}{2n} = \frac{2}{3}-\frac{1}{6n}$, namely a supermajority greater-or-equal to $\frac{2}{3}n$ is required.
%% Cut for DISC
%; and if everyone but one agent is faulty then $\sigma = \frac{2n-1}{2n} = 1-\frac{1}{2n}$, namely unanimity is required.  

Under this assumption, a $\sigma$-supermajority includes a majority of the correct agents, and hence
the intersection of any two $\sigma$-supermajorities  includes at least one correct agent.
Typically, permissioned consensus protocols refer to ``blocks by at least $n-f$ agents among $P$'', which is equivalent to ``a $\sigma$-supermajority among $P$'' in the standard case of  $n = 3f+1$. 

%
\mypara{Waves} A blocklace is seen as a sequence of waves, where the  \emph{$k^{th}$-wave} in $B$ , $k\ge 1$, consists of three consecutive rounds $r$, $r+1$, $r+2$ in $B$,   $r=3(k-1)+1$, referred to as the \emph{first}, \emph{second}, and \emph{third round} of the wave.

For a given $P\subset \Pi$, the partial function \emph{leader$_P$} assigns a leader $p\in P$ for the first round of every wave, where a first-round $p$-block $b$ 
with prevailing constitution $(P,\sigma)$
is a \emph{leader block} if $p=\textit{leader}_P(d(b))$. We assume the leader function to be \emph{fair} in that for every wave and any $p\in P$ there is a subsequent wave for which $p$ is the leader of its first round.

%\idit{For liveness, we need more from  the leader function. First,  it must take $P$ as a parameter (implicit or explicit) and return some  $p \in P$, because $\Pi$ can be infinite. Second, it must satisfy some fairness property to ensure that not only Byzantine agents are leaders.}\udi{OK now?}

The consensus protocol progresses in waves.  In each wave, a first round block is a candidate for finality in two cases---if it is a leader block or the only block in its round. These two possibilities typically arise in \emph{high- and low-throughput} scenarios, respectively. During low throughput, agents quiesce, not producing a first-round block until they have a new transaction; and when an agent produces a first-round block $b$ that does not conflict with others, all agents produce second and third round blocks that finalize $b$. During high throughput, conflicting blocks may be produced and the leader function is used to break symmetry;  
%During high throughput,  
leader blocks are finalized and induce the  total ordering of all blocks. 

A second-round block $b$ of a wave may approve any number of first-round blocks, but \emph{endorses} (towards finality) at most one of them,  defined as follows.  Note that the next two definitions are mutually-recursive:

\begin{definition}[Endorse]
Let $b$ be a first-round block 
with prevailing constitution $(P,\sigma)$
and $b'$ a second-round block of the same wave in  $B$. Then $b'$ \temph{endorses} $b$ if the previous wave is:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{quiescent} and 
        $b$ is the only block in its round approved by $b'$, or 
        %\andy{The term `quiescent' is not yet defined. If this sort of recursive definition is required, best to warn the reader and explicitly point out that `quiescence' for earlier rounds will be defined below.}\udi{corrected}
    \item \textbf{non-quiescent} and $b$ is a leader block approved by $b'$. 
\end{enumerate}
A set of blocks $D$ \emph{endorses} $b$ if every $b'\in D$ endorses $b$.
\label{definition:endorse}
\end{definition}

A first-round block $b$ is final if the third round of the same wave has a $\sigma$-supermajority that approves a second-round $\sigma$-supermajority that endorses $b$. Formally (text in parenthesis is explanatory, the definition is valid also without it):

\begin{definition}[Ratified, Final, Ordered, Quiescent]\label{definition:final} 
Given  a blocklace $B$,  a block $b\in B$ with prevailing constitution $(P,\sigma)$ is:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \temph{ratified} by a block $b'$ if $b'$ approves a  $\sigma$-supermajority that endorses $b$;
%    \idit{There is a lack of symmetry in the definitions. We say above that $B$ endorses $b$ if \emph{every} $b' \in B$ endorses it,  and now that $B$ ratifies $b$ if there is \emph{some} $b' \in B$ does. This is confusing. }\udi{corrected}
    \item \temph{final} in $B$ if %$b$ is a first-round block and 
    there is a  $\sigma$-supermajority in the third-round of $b$'s wave in $B$, in which each block ratifies $b$, referred to as a \temph{finalizing $\sigma$-supermajority}.

%\idit{I moved the round constraints from the definition of ratified to that of final because the proof uses ratify without round restrictions (any later block can ratify while finalization is only in the current wave).}
%\item \temph{ordered in} $B$ if $b$ is approved by a (first round) block $b'$ final in $B$, in which case we also say that $b$ is \emph{ordered by} $b'$ in $B$. 
%\idit{Do we need the definition of $b$ ordered by $b'$? Note that this is a one-to-many relation, since all ensuing final blocks finalize $b'$ as well. Again, confusing, not sure why we need to define it.}
\end{enumerate} 
A wave in such a blocklace $B$ is: 
\begin{enumerate}
