During low-throughput, in the good-case scenario, all waves are quiescent.  A wave commences when some agent has a new transaction. This agent issues a (first-round) non-empty block, and all agents follow by issuing two rounds of blocks: Second-round blocks that endorse the first-round block, and third-round blocks each approving all second-round blocks, upon which the first-round block becomes final.  The new wave is also quiescent provided all second- and third-round blocks are empty. The agent that issued a finalized, non-empty, first-round block in this scenario is referred to as a \emph{spontaneous leader}, in contrast with the \emph{formal leader} assigned by the \emph{leader$_P$} function. 

During high-throughput, no wave is quiescent, and the good-case scenario unrolls as above, except that the first-round block is issued promptly by the wave's (formal) leader, whether or not it has any payload, and second- and third-round blocks are typically non-empty (contain transactions to be ordered by the next-wave's final leader block).  The good-case scenarios are summarized in Figure \ref{fig:wave}. 


Going beyond the good case,  if during high-throughput agents fail to receive a first-round leader block in a timely manner, they issue first-round blocks of their own. Once a super-majority of agents do so, agents can continue to the next round  without waiting for a leader block.
%Correct  agents issue second round blocks that endorse the first round leader block and approve other blocks, if they receive any. Finally, correct agents issue a third round block that approves all the endorsing blocks; if a supermajority of third-round block each approves a supermajority of second-round blocks that endorse the leader block, then it becomes final. 
%If the blocklace is quiescent, the first round of the next wave is silent until some agent has payload to issue. If a single agent issues a first-round block, this block is endorsed by correct agents and then becomes final as in the non-quiescent case. 
And if during low-throughput there are multiple agents issuing first-round blocks, then it is possible that none of the first round blocks will become final.  In this case, the wave is not quiescent and the protocol proceeds as in the high-throughput scenario. 

To formalize these progress rules, we define 
a round $r$ as \emph{advanced} in a blocklace $B$ if $B$ includes 
enough blocks to allow round $r+1$ blocks to be added to the blocklace safely:

\begin{definition}[Advanced Round]\label{definition:advanced-complete} 
Given a blocklace $B$ with prevailing constitution $(P,\sigma)$,  a round $r>0$ in $B$ is \temph{advanced} if $r$ is a:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item first round that either  (\ia) includes a  $\sigma$-supermajority of blocks among $P$, or (\ib) includes a leader block or  (\ic) follows a quiescent wave and includes at least one block, or a
    \item second or third round that includes a  $\sigma$-supermajority of blocks among $P$.
\end{enumerate}
Round $0$ is trivially advanced in all blocklaces.
\end{definition}

Agents incorporate blocks in the blocklace only if they are valid, namely could have been produced by a correct agent during the run of the protocol. Formally, an ordinary block $b$ is \emph{valid} if it is well-formed and round $d(b)-1$ is advanced in $[b]$.


