If more than one block in the wave is non-empty, the wave is deemed non-quiescent, and the protocol switches to high throughput mode in the ensuing wave. 

In high throughput mode, we use the leader function to break the symmetry among multiple senders. Only the round's leader sends a first-round block (regardless of whether it has payload or not). Other agents wait for the leader's block, and if the leader is deemed unresponsive,  send their own blocks. The protocol advances to the second round once either a leader block or a super-majority of blocks is accepted.  

In the second and third rounds, all agents issue blocks, and the round advances on receiving a super-majority. A second round block endorses at most one block, the single sent block in low throughput mode and the leader block in high throughput mode. A third round block ratifies at most one first round block.
