\begin{definition}[Valid Constitutional Amendment]\label{definition:valid-amendment}
Given a sequence of constitutional amendment decisions $d_1,d_2,\ldots$, $d_i$ is \temph{valid} if $i=1$ and $Q_1 = P_1$, or $i>1$,  $d_{i-1}$ is valid, and $Q_i$ is a $\max(\sigma_{i-1},\sigma_i)$-supermajority in $P_{i-1}$ and a $\max(\sigma_{i-1},\sigma_i)$-supermajority in $P_i$. The sequence is \temph{valid} if all its members are valid.
Two non-identical constitutional amendment decisions are \temph{conflicting} if they have the same index.
\end{definition}
Note that the two consecutive supermajorities are possibly overlapping, even identical.
In the special case that only $\Delta_i$  changes,  a $\sigma_i$-supermajority among $P_i$ is needed; if only $\sigma_i$  changes then a single $\max(\sigma_{i_1},\sigma_i)$-supermajority among $P_i$ is needed; and if only $P_i$  changes then a $\sigma_i$-supermajority among $P_{i-1}$ and a $\sigma_i$-supermajority among $P_i$ is needed.

This definition ensures that the $h$-rule is followed and that the `dead sailors' scenario of Paxos (that the new $P'$ does not exists)~\cite{lamport10.1145/279227.279229part-time} has not happened.


We assume that correct agents do not sign invalid or two conflicting constitutional amendment decisions, and hence can make the following observation.
\begin{observation}[Safety of Constitutional Amendment]
Given a valid sequence of constitutional amendment decisions $d_1,\ldots d_i$, $i\ge 1$, if the number $f$ of faulty agents in $P_i$ among the total number of agents $n=|P_i|$ satisfies $\sigma_i \ge \frac{n+f}{2n}$,  then the constitutional amendment process cannot extend this sequence with two conflicting valid constitutional amendment decisions.  
\end{observation}
