\section{Unreliable Network}

One intended application for Grassroots Consensus is the running of social contracts among people armed with smartphones.  We expect such use to engage mostly the low-throughput mode of Grassroots Consensus.

Despite smartphones being thousands of times more powerful in terms of computing, memory, and networking compared to early Web servers, they are not ``first class'' citizens on the net as  there are numerous hurdles for smartphone peer-to-peer communication.  One of them is establishing a direct TCP connection, and re-establishing it once the IP address of one of the phones changes due to roaming.
Hence, a UDP-based protocol would be much preferable over the TCP-based protocol for smartphone peer-to-peer applications.  The formal implication of this is forgoing the assumption that the network is reliable also before GST.  

Here, we adjust the Grassroots Consensus protocol to operate over an unreliable network.

