Despite the advantages of NFV, establishing adaptive resource slices for VSPs remains a challenging issue. 
One of the fundamental dilemmas pertains to the competitive dynamics for resources among various VSPs, necessitating that NSPs devise equitable and efficacious mechanisms for resource allocation to foster the active participation of VSPs in resource negotiation. 
Furthermore, given that NSPs often embody public benefit attributes, their optimization goals transcend mere equity in resource distribution, incorporating the maximization of social welfare (the sum of all VSPs' utility). However, the pursuit of societal welfare enhancement may clash with the individual profit-maximization goals of VSPs, thereby adding layers of complexity to the allocation quandary. Such problems are typically modeled within the frameworks of game theory, and auction-based mechanism design emerges as a potential resolution, celebrated for its merits in incentive compatibility and operational efficiency.
