\section{Conclusion}
% In this paper, a resource-slicing allocation problem is discussed in IoV. We propose a novel VoI-based dual-currency VCG auction mechanism, skillfully engineered to elevate social welfare by optimizing resource allocation. This innovative means is particularly effective in preserving the privacy of VSPs and diluting potential collusion risks, all while exhibiting DSIC properties. Notably, it significantly pares down the volume of average information exchange during each auction iteration. As for future research directions, we envision delving into more complex networks to tackle resource allocation conundrums, thereby broadening the horizons of this current discourse.

This paper addresses the resource-slicing allocation problem in IoV by proposing a novel VoI-based dual-currency VCG auction mechanism. The mechanism achieves near-optimal social welfare improvement through optimized resource allocation without requiring knowledge of VSPs' revenue models. We theoretically and experimentally demonstrate that the mechanism satisfies DSIC properties, and can effectively mitigate potential collusion risks among bidders. 
Additionally, the mechanism significantly reduces the volume of information exchange per auction iteration while maintaining acceptable computational complexity, making it suitable for practical applications. 
% In the future, we will dive into more complex networks like A to tackle resource allocation conundrums, thereby broadening the horizons of this current discourse.
In the future, we plan to extend our research to broader practical network scenarios and real-world optimization challenges. A representative focus will be solving multidimensional resource allocation problems in MIMO systems under complex constraints across frequency, temporal, spatial, and power domains, thereby broadening the horizons of this current discourse.

